/* AI brain Q&A pack 23: devices, internet & tech help */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["how does a touchscreen work | how do touch screens work | touchscreen", "Most phone screens are **capacitive**: a grid of tiny electrodes holds an electric charge, and your finger (which conducts electricity) changes it at the spot you touch, so the device knows where you tapped."],
  ["why doesnt my touchscreen work with gloves | touchscreen gloves | resistive vs capacitive screen", "Capacitive screens need something that conducts electricity, like skin, so ordinary gloves block the signal. **Touchscreen gloves** have conductive thread in the fingertips. Older **resistive** screens worked by pressure instead, so any object could press them."],
  ["how does a stylus work | what is an active stylus | stylus vs finger", "A simple rubber-tipped stylus just imitates a fingertip on a capacitive screen. An **active stylus** has its own electronics, so the device can sense its exact position, pressure and tilt, which is great for drawing and handwriting."],
  ["what does multi touch mean | how does pinch to zoom work | multitouch", "Multi-touch means the screen can track several fingers at once. The device measures how the distance between two fingers changes, so spreading them zooms in and pinching them zooms out."],
  ["how does a phone battery work | how do rechargeable batteries work", "Phones and laptops use **lithium-ion** batteries. When charging, lithium ions move from one side of the battery to the other and store energy; when you use the device, they flow back and release that energy as electricity."],
  ["what does mah mean | what is battery capacity | mah meaning", "**mAh** (milliamp-hours) measures how much charge a battery can hold. A bigger number usually means longer battery life, but how efficient the device is matters just as much."],
  ["should i let my battery die before charging | is it bad to charge to 100 percent | how do i keep my battery healthy", "No need to drain it! Lithium-ion batteries are happiest kept roughly between 20% and 80%, and away from heat. Many phones have an **optimized charging** setting that waits to finish the last bit until you need it."],
  ["is it bad to charge my phone overnight | can you overcharge a phone | can i leave my phone plugged in", "Modern phones stop charging when full, so you can't really overcharge them. The bigger enemy is **heat**, so charge on a hard surface, never under a pillow, and turn on optimized charging if your phone has it."],
  ["what is fast charging | how does fast charging work | why do some chargers charge faster", "Fast charging pushes more power (watts) into the battery while it's low. The phone and charger talk to each other to agree on a safe amount, then slow down as the battery fills up to protect it."],
  ["how does wireless charging work | what is qi charging | how do wireless chargers work", "A wireless charger has a coil of wire that makes a changing magnetic field. A matching coil inside your phone turns that field back into electricity (**electromagnetic induction**). It must be lined up well, and it's a bit slower and warmer than a cable."],
  ["why does my battery percentage jump | battery percentage is wrong | phone died at 20 percent", "The percentage is an estimate. Cold weather, an old battery or heavy use can make the reading drop suddenly. If it happens a lot, check **battery health** in settings; an aging battery may need replacing."],
  ["why do batteries wear out | what is battery health | battery health percentage", "Every charge cycle causes tiny chemical changes, so a lithium-ion battery slowly holds less charge. **Battery health** shows how much capacity is left compared to new. Heat and constantly sitting at 0% or 100% speed up the wear."],
  ["why does my phone get warm when charging | is it normal for a charger to get hot | charger getting warm", "A little warmth is normal, because some energy turns into heat, especially with fast or wireless charging. If it gets too hot to hold, unplug it, take off thick cases, and use a good-quality charger."],
  ["what is a power bank | how does a portable charger work | can i take a power bank on a plane", "A power bank is a battery in a box that you charge up and then use to charge your phone later. Airlines usually let you bring one in your **carry-on** bag but not in checked luggage, because lithium batteries can be a fire risk."],
  ["what does watts mean on a charger | volts amps and watts | what is wattage", "**Watts** measure power: volts times amps. A higher-watt charger can charge compatible devices faster, but your device only takes as much power as it safely can."],
  ["can i use any usb c charger | is it safe to use a different charger | can i use my laptop charger for my phone", "Usually yes. Good USB-C chargers and devices talk to each other (using **USB Power Delivery**) and agree on a safe power level. Stick to trusted, certified chargers and cables; very cheap no-name ones can be unsafe."],
  ["what is a gan charger | why are some chargers so small | gallium nitride charger", "**GaN** (gallium nitride) chargers use a newer kind of chip that wastes less energy as heat, so they can be smaller and lighter while still delivering lots of power."],
  ["what are the types of usb | usb a usb b micro usb | different usb cables", "Common USB plugs:\n1. **USB-A**: the classic flat rectangle.\n2. **USB-B**: a squarish plug, often on printers.\n3. **Micro-USB**: small, found on older phones and gadgets.\n4. **USB-C**: small, oval and reversible, used on most new devices."],
  ["what does usb stand for | usb meaning", "USB stands for **Universal Serial Bus**. It was designed so lots of different devices (keyboards, drives, phones and more) could connect with one standard kind of port."],
  ["what is thunderbolt | is usb c the same as thunderbolt | thunderbolt vs usb c", "**Thunderbolt** uses the same USB-C shaped plug, but it supports extra-fast data, multiple monitors and docks. Every Thunderbolt port works with USB-C devices, but not every USB-C port supports Thunderbolt; look for the lightning-bolt symbol."],
  ["why are some usb c cables slow | usb c cable charges but no data | usb 2 vs usb 3", "Cables that look the same can be very different inside. Some USB-C cables only support charging and slow **USB 2** data speeds, while others support fast **USB 3** or Thunderbolt data and high-power charging. Check the cable's labels or packaging."],
  ["what is a usb hub | what is a docking station | not enough usb ports", "A **USB hub** turns one port into several. A **docking station** goes further, adding ports like HDMI, Ethernet and charging, so a laptop can connect to a whole desk setup with one cable."],
  ["how do i pair a bluetooth device | how to connect a bluetooth speaker | bluetooth pairing steps", "1. Turn on Bluetooth on your phone or computer.\n2. Put the speaker or headphones in **pairing mode** (often by holding the power or Bluetooth button until a light flashes).\n3. Pick the device from the Bluetooth list.\n4. Confirm any code if asked. After the first time, it usually reconnects automatically."],
  ["how far does bluetooth reach | bluetooth range | why does bluetooth cut out", "Most Bluetooth devices work up to about **10 meters (30 feet)**, less through walls or bodies. Walls, microwaves and crowded Wi-Fi can cause dropouts, so move closer if the sound starts skipping."],
  ["can bluetooth connect to two devices | what is multipoint bluetooth | connect headphones to phone and laptop", "Some headphones support **multipoint**, which lets them stay connected to two devices at once, like a phone and a laptop, and switch automatically. Without it, you need to disconnect from one before connecting to the other."],
  ["what is a bluetooth codec | what is aptx | which bluetooth audio codec is best", "A **codec** is the method used to squeeze audio so it fits over Bluetooth. Common ones include SBC, AAC, aptX and LDAC. Better codecs can sound clearer, but both devices must support the same one."],
  ["why is my bluetooth audio delayed | bluetooth lag | audio is behind the video", "Bluetooth needs a moment to compress and send sound, which can cause a small delay. Many video apps fix this automatically. For gaming, try a **low-latency mode**, a wireless dongle or wired headphones."],
  ["2.4 ghz vs 5 ghz wifi | which wifi band should i use | what is 2.4ghz", "**2.4 GHz** reaches farther and goes through walls better but is slower and more crowded. **5 GHz** (and 6 GHz on newer routers) is much faster but has shorter range. Use 5 GHz when you're close to the router."],
  ["what is mesh wifi | do i need mesh wifi | mesh network vs router", "A **mesh** system uses several little routers placed around the home that work together as one network, so you get strong Wi-Fi in every room. It's handy for big or multi-story homes with dead spots."],
  ["what is a wifi extender | wifi booster | extender vs mesh", "An **extender** picks up your router's signal and repeats it farther out. It's cheap and simple but can cut speed and may create a separate network name. Mesh systems usually do the same job more smoothly."],
  ["where should i put my router | best place for a wifi router | router placement", "Put the router up high, near the middle of your home and out in the open. Keep it away from thick walls, metal, fish tanks and microwaves, and not hidden inside a cabinet."],
  ["what is wifi 6 | what do wifi numbers mean | wifi 5 vs wifi 6", "Wi-Fi standards get version numbers. Higher numbers are newer and can be faster and better at handling many devices at once. Your router and device both need to support a version to use its features."],
  ["what is an ssid | what is my wifi name | ssid meaning", "The **SSID** is just your Wi-Fi network's name, the one you pick from the list when connecting. You can change it in your router's settings."],
  ["what is wpa2 | what is wpa3 | wifi security types", "WPA2 and WPA3 are the security systems that encrypt your Wi-Fi so neighbors can't snoop or hop on. **WPA3** is newer and stronger. Avoid old **WEP** or open networks with no password at home."],
  ["how do i find my wifi password | forgot my wifi password | where is the wifi password", "Look on the sticker on the router; the default password is often printed there. A device already connected can often show or share it in its Wi-Fi settings. If it was changed, ask whoever set up the router."],
  ["what is a hotspot | how do i use my phone as a hotspot | what is tethering", "A **hotspot** shares your phone's mobile data as a Wi-Fi network so a laptop or tablet can get online. Turn it on in your phone's settings. It uses your data plan, so watch out for big downloads."],
  ["how do cell phones work | what is a cell tower | how does a phone call travel", "Your phone sends and receives radio waves to nearby **cell towers**. Each tower covers an area called a cell, and as you move, your phone is handed from tower to tower. The towers connect to the wider phone and internet network."],
  ["what does lte mean | is lte the same as 4g", "**LTE** (Long Term Evolution) is the main technology behind 4G mobile networks. When your phone shows LTE, it's basically using 4G."],
  ["is 5g safe | does 5g cause health problems | is 5g dangerous", "5G uses radio waves, the same general kind used by radio, TV and older phone networks. They are **non-ionizing**, meaning they can't damage DNA, and health agencies have found no evidence that 5G at allowed levels harms people."],
  ["why is 5g faster | 5g vs 4g speed | what makes 5g fast", "5G can use more radio frequencies, including very high ones that carry lots of data, plus smarter antennas. That gives faster speeds and lower delay, though the fastest kinds have shorter range and are blocked by walls more easily."],
  ["what is a sim card | what is an esim | esim vs sim card", "A **SIM** identifies your phone number and plan to the mobile network. A physical SIM is a tiny card; an **eSIM** is built into the phone and set up digitally, so you can switch plans without swapping cards."],
  ["what is roaming | what is data roaming | should i turn off roaming", "**Roaming** means your phone is using another company's network, usually when you travel abroad. It can cost extra, so many people turn data roaming off when traveling unless their plan includes it."],
  ["why do i have no signal | no service on my phone | what do the bars on my phone mean", "The bars show how strong your connection to the cell tower is. Signal drops in basements, elevators, rural areas or thick buildings. Try moving near a window, toggling airplane mode on and off, or restarting the phone."],
  ["what is wifi calling | how does wifi calling work", "**Wi-Fi calling** lets your phone make normal calls and texts over a Wi-Fi connection instead of a cell tower. It's useful where mobile signal is weak but Wi-Fi is good."],
  ["what is airplane mode for | why do planes need airplane mode | does airplane mode save battery", "Airplane mode turns off the phone's radios, like cellular, so it doesn't transmit during flights. It also saves battery, because searching for a weak signal uses lots of power. You can usually turn Wi-Fi back on separately."],
  ["how does gps work | how does my phone know where i am | how does gps find location", "GPS satellites orbit Earth sending out signals that say exactly what time it is. Your phone hears several of them and uses the tiny differences in arrival time to **calculate** its position. That's called trilateration."],
  ["does gps need internet | can gps work offline | does gps use data", "GPS itself works without internet, because it only listens to satellites. But map apps need data to load maps and traffic, so download **offline maps** before a trip if you won't have signal."],
  ["why is my gps wrong | gps location inaccurate | gps keeps jumping", "Tall buildings, tunnels, thick trees and being indoors can block or bounce satellite signals. Turn on Wi-Fi and high-accuracy location, step outside, or recalibrate the compass to improve it."],
  ["how many gps satellites are there | are there other satellite navigation systems | what is galileo", "The GPS system uses around **30** satellites. There are other systems too, such as Europe's Galileo, Russia's GLONASS and China's BeiDou, and most phones listen to several for better accuracy."],
  ["how do qr codes work | how do i scan a qr code | what does a qr code store", "A QR code stores information, often a web link, in a grid of black and white squares. Point your phone camera at it and a link pops up. The three big squares in the corners help the camera line it up from any angle."],
  ["are qr codes safe | can a qr code be a scam | is it safe to scan qr codes", "The code itself can't hurt you, but it can lead to a fake website. Check the link preview before opening, be careful of stickers placed over real codes, and never type passwords or payment details from a random QR code."],
  ["how do barcodes work | how does a barcode scanner work | what do barcode numbers mean", "A barcode is a number written as black bars and white gaps of different widths. A scanner shines light and reads the pattern of reflections, then looks the number up in a database to find the product and price."],
  ["barcode vs qr code | difference between a barcode and a qr code", "A normal barcode is one-dimensional and holds a short number. A **QR code** is two-dimensional, so it can hold much more, like a whole web address or text, and can still be read if part of it is damaged."],
  ["what is nfc | how does nfc work | near field communication", "**NFC** is a very short-range wireless technology that works when two devices are a few centimeters apart. It's used for tap-to-pay, transit cards, and quickly pairing gadgets."],
  ["how does tap to pay work | is contactless payment safe | how does paying with a phone work", "Tap to pay uses NFC to send a one-time code to the card reader. Phone wallets don't share your real card number; they use a stand-in number called a **token**, plus your fingerprint, face or PIN, which makes it quite secure."],
  ["what is rfid | rfid vs nfc | how do security tags work", "**RFID** tags are tiny chips with antennas that a reader can power and read using radio waves. They're used for shop security tags, pet microchips and warehouse tracking. NFC is a short-range type of RFID."],
  ["how does a digital camera work | how does a phone camera take pictures | what is an image sensor", "The lens focuses light onto an **image sensor** covered in millions of tiny light detectors. Each detector measures how much light hits it, and the camera's processor turns those measurements into a picture."],
  ["what are megapixels | do more megapixels mean better photos | what does mp mean on a camera", "One megapixel is a million pixels. More megapixels give more detail for cropping or big prints, but the **sensor size**, lens and processing usually matter more for overall photo quality."],
  ["why do phones have multiple cameras | what is a wide angle lens | what is a telephoto lens", "Each camera has a different lens: **ultra-wide** fits more in, **main** is the all-rounder, and **telephoto** zooms in. Some extra sensors help with depth for portrait shots."],
  ["optical zoom vs digital zoom | why does zooming make photos blurry | what is digital zoom", "**Optical zoom** uses the lens to magnify, so quality stays sharp. **Digital zoom** just enlarges part of the image, like cropping, so it gets blurry and blocky. Moving closer is often the best zoom."],
  ["what is hdr on a camera | hdr photo mode | what does hdr do in photos", "HDR (high dynamic range) mode takes several shots at different brightness levels and blends them, so bright skies and dark shadows both show detail in one photo."],
  ["how does night mode work | night mode on phone camera | why do night photos take longer", "Night mode takes many photos over a second or more and combines them with smart processing, brightening the scene and reducing grain. Hold still or rest the phone on something while it works."],
  ["what is portrait mode | how does the blurry background work | what is bokeh", "Portrait mode figures out what's close and what's far, then blurs the background to make the subject pop. That soft blur is called **bokeh**. Real cameras do it with big lenses; phones mostly do it with software."],
  ["what is image stabilization | what is ois | why are my videos shaky", "Stabilization reduces wobble. **Optical** stabilization moves the lens or sensor to cancel shakes, and **electronic** stabilization crops and shifts the video. Holding the phone with two hands helps too."],
  ["what is aperture | what is shutter speed | what is iso", "These three control exposure:\n1. **Aperture**: how wide the lens opens to let light in.\n2. **Shutter speed**: how long the sensor collects light.\n3. **ISO**: how sensitive the sensor is (higher is brighter but grainier)."],
  ["what is a raw photo | raw vs jpeg | should i shoot in raw", "A **RAW** file keeps all the data straight from the sensor, giving more room to fix colors and brightness later, but the files are big. **JPEG** is processed and compressed, so it's smaller and ready to share."],
  ["30fps vs 60fps video | what frame rate should i record video in | what is frame rate in video", "Frame rate is how many pictures per second a video has. **30 fps** looks normal, **60 fps** looks smoother for fast action, and high frame rates like 120 or 240 can be slowed down for slow motion."],
  ["how do speakers work | how does a speaker make sound", "An electrical audio signal flows through a coil attached to a cone. A magnet pushes and pulls the coil, making the cone vibrate. Those vibrations push the air, creating the sound waves you hear."],
  ["over ear vs in ear | earbuds vs headphones | which headphones should i get", "**Over-ear** headphones are comfy for long sessions and block more noise. **On-ear** are lighter. **Earbuds** are tiny and portable. Pick based on where you'll use them most."],
  ["how does noise cancelling work | what is anc | active noise cancellation", "Microphones on the headphones listen to outside noise, then the headphones play the exact **opposite** sound wave. The two waves cancel each other out, which works best on steady sounds like engines and fans."],
  ["what is transparency mode | what is ambient mode on headphones | hear around me mode", "Transparency mode uses the headphones' microphones to let outside sounds in, so you can hear announcements, traffic or someone talking without taking the earbuds out."],
  ["are loud headphones bad for your ears | how loud should headphones be | what is the 60 60 rule", "Yes, loud music for long periods can permanently damage hearing. Try the **60/60 rule**: no more than 60% volume for about 60 minutes at a time, then take a break. If others can hear your music, it's too loud."],
  ["what is a subwoofer | what is a tweeter | what makes bass sound", "Speakers split up the job: a **subwoofer** handles deep bass, a **woofer** covers low-middle sounds, and a **tweeter** handles high notes. Bigger cones move more air, which is why bass speakers are big."],
  ["what is surround sound | what is spatial audio | what does 5.1 mean", "**Surround sound** uses several speakers around you; **5.1** means five speakers plus one subwoofer. **Spatial audio** uses clever processing to make sound seem to come from all around, even through headphones."],
  ["how does a microphone work | what is a condenser mic | how do mics pick up sound", "Sound waves make a thin surface called a **diaphragm** vibrate, and the microphone turns those vibrations into an electrical signal. Dynamic mics are tough; condenser mics are more sensitive and detailed."],
  ["why is my mic making a loud squeal | microphone feedback | why does my mic echo", "That squeal is **feedback**: the mic picks up sound from the speaker, which gets amplified again and again. Turn the speaker down, move the mic away from it, or use headphones."],
  ["wired vs wireless headphones | are wired headphones better", "Wired headphones never need charging and have zero delay, which is great for music making and gaming. Wireless ones are more convenient and freer to move in. Sound quality can be great with either."],
  ["how does a microwave oven work | how do microwaves heat food", "A microwave oven makes invisible waves that make **water molecules** in food wiggle back and forth super fast. That jiggling creates heat, which cooks the food from the inside and outside at once."],
  ["why cant you put metal in the microwave | why does metal spark in the microwave", "Metal reflects microwaves, and sharp edges like foil or fork prongs can build up electric charge and create **sparks**, which can damage the oven or start a fire. Use microwave-safe dishes instead."],
  ["why does microwave food heat unevenly | cold spots in microwave food | why does the microwave have a turntable", "Microwaves bounce around and create hot and cold spots. The **turntable** moves food through them. Stir halfway, spread food out evenly, and let it rest a minute so heat spreads."],
  ["how do smart speakers work | is my smart speaker always listening | how does a voice assistant hear me", "A smart speaker listens locally for its **wake word**. Only after it hears it does it start recording and send your request online to be understood. Most have a mute button to switch the mic off."],
  ["what is a smart home | what is a smart plug | what are smart home devices", "A smart home has devices you can control from a phone or by voice, like **smart plugs**, lights, thermostats and doorbells. They connect over Wi-Fi or other wireless systems and can run on schedules."],
  ["what is iot | what is the internet of things", "The **Internet of Things** means everyday objects, like thermostats, fridges, watches and cameras, that connect to the internet to send data or be controlled remotely."],
  ["how does a smartwatch work | what can a smartwatch do | smartwatch vs fitness tracker", "A smartwatch is a tiny computer on your wrist that connects to your phone. It can show notifications, track exercise and sleep, and run apps. A **fitness tracker** is simpler, focusing mainly on activity and health."],
  ["how does a smartwatch measure heart rate | how does a fitness tracker count steps", "Green lights on the back shine into your skin; blood absorbs green light, so the sensor sees tiny changes with each heartbeat. Steps are counted by an **accelerometer** that feels the rhythm of your arm swinging."],
  ["what is e-ink | how does an e-reader work | e-ink vs lcd", "E-readers use **e-ink**: tiny capsules of black and white particles that move up or down to form text. It reflects light like paper instead of glowing, so it's easy on the eyes and readable in sunshine."],
  ["why do e-readers last so long | e-reader battery life", "E-ink only uses power when the page **changes**. A still page uses almost nothing, so an e-reader can go weeks between charges."],
  ["how does a game console work | console vs gaming pc | what is inside a game console", "A console is a computer specially built for games, with a CPU, GPU, memory and storage designed to work together. It's simple to set up and play, while a gaming PC is more customizable and upgradable."],
  ["how does virtual reality work | what is a vr headset | how does vr trick your brain", "A VR headset puts a screen in front of each eye showing slightly different images, which creates **3D depth**. Sensors track your head movements and update the view instantly, so it feels like you're inside the world."],
  ["what is augmented reality | ar vs vr | what is mixed reality", "**VR** replaces everything you see with a virtual world. **AR** adds digital things on top of the real world, like filters or games through your phone camera. **Mixed reality** blends both, letting virtual objects interact with your room."],
  ["why does vr make me feel sick | vr motion sickness | how to stop vr sickness", "Motion sickness happens when your eyes see movement but your body feels still. Start with short sessions, choose comfort settings like teleport movement, and stop as soon as you feel queasy."],
  ["is vr safe for kids | how old do you have to be for vr | vr age limit", "Many headset makers set a minimum age, often around 10 to 13, so check the box or manual. Take regular breaks, clear space so you don't bump into things, and let a grown-up help set it up."],
  ["how does a 3d printer work | what is 3d printing | what is filament", "Most home 3D printers melt plastic **filament** and squeeze it out through a hot nozzle, building an object one thin layer at a time from a digital model. Bigger objects can take many hours."],
  ["what can you make with a 3d printer | what are 3d printers used for", "People 3D print toys, figurines, replacement parts, phone stands, cosplay props, prototypes and even medical models. Engineers use them to test ideas quickly before making real products."],
  ["fdm vs resin printer | what is resin 3d printing", "**FDM** printers melt plastic filament and are easy to start with. **Resin** printers use liquid resin hardened by UV light, giving super-fine detail, but the resin needs gloves and good ventilation, so it's best with adult help."],
  ["how do drones fly | how does a quadcopter work | how do drones stay in the air", "A quadcopter has four spinning propellers. By speeding up or slowing down certain ones, it can rise, fall, tilt and turn. Built-in sensors and a tiny computer constantly balance it so it hovers steadily."],
  ["drone rules | do you need a license to fly a drone | where can i fly a drone", "Rules depend on your country. Generally: keep the drone in sight, stay away from airports, crowds and private property, and don't fly too high. Heavier drones often need to be **registered**. Check your local aviation rules first."],
  ["how does an ev work | what is an electric vehicle", "An electric car stores energy in a big battery and uses it to power electric motors that turn the wheels. There's no gas engine, so it's quiet, has fewer moving parts and produces no exhaust while driving."],
  ["how do you charge an electric car | how long does it take to charge an ev | ev charging levels", "You can charge at home from a wall outlet (slow, overnight) or a home charger (faster), or at public **fast chargers** that can add lots of range in under an hour. Time depends on the battery size and charger power."],
  ["hybrid vs electric | what is a plug in hybrid", "A **hybrid** has both a gas engine and an electric motor that share the work. A **plug-in hybrid** has a bigger battery you can charge to drive short trips on electricity. A fully **electric** car has no engine at all."],
  ["what is regenerative braking | how do electric cars recharge when braking", "When an electric car slows down, its motor works in reverse like a generator, turning the car's motion back into electricity to top up the battery. It saves energy and wears the brakes less."],
  ["how do solar panels make electricity | what is a solar cell", "Solar panels are made of **solar cells**, usually silicon. When sunlight hits them, it knocks electrons loose and creates an electric current. An **inverter** then converts it into the kind of power your home uses."],
  ["do solar panels work at night | do solar panels need direct sun | solar panels when its cloudy", "They still make some power on cloudy days, just less. At night they make none, so homes use stored battery power or electricity from the grid instead."],
  ["what is a home battery | what is a solar inverter | how do homes store solar power", "A home battery stores extra solar energy made during the day so you can use it at night or during a power cut. The **inverter** converts the panels' DC electricity into AC electricity for your outlets."],
  ["oled vs led tv | what is oled | difference between oled and led", "In an **OLED** screen, every pixel makes its own light and can switch off completely, giving perfect blacks and great contrast. An **LED** TV is an LCD screen lit from behind by LEDs; it can get very bright but blacks look more gray."],
  ["what is qled | what is mini led | what is an lcd screen", "An **LCD** uses liquid crystals to block or let through light from a backlight. **Mini LED** uses thousands of tiny backlight LEDs for better contrast, and **QLED** adds a quantum-dot layer for brighter, richer colors. All are types of LCD."],
  ["what is hdr on a tv | what does hdr mean on a tv | hdr10 vs dolby vision", "HDR (high dynamic range) lets a TV show brighter highlights, deeper shadows and more colors, so scenes look closer to real life. HDR10, HDR10+ and Dolby Vision are different HDR formats; you need HDR content and a TV that supports it."],
  ["4k vs 8k | is 8k worth it | what is 8k", "4K has about 8 million pixels; 8K has about 33 million. On most screen sizes and seating distances, your eyes can barely tell the difference, and there's not much 8K content, so 4K is plenty for most people."],
  ["what size tv should i get | how far should i sit from the tv | tv size for room", "A common guide for a 4K TV is to sit about **1 to 1.5 times** the screen's diagonal size away. So for a 55-inch TV, roughly 1.5 to 2 meters (5 to 7 feet) works well."],
  ["what is motion smoothing | what is the soap opera effect | 120hz tv", "A **120Hz** TV refreshes twice as often as 60Hz, making motion smoother. **Motion smoothing** invents extra frames, which can make movies look oddly like cheap video (the soap opera effect). You can switch it off in picture settings."],
  ["what is a smart tv | how does a smart tv work", "A smart TV has a built-in computer and internet connection, so it can run streaming apps, browse and sometimes use voice control without a separate box."],
  ["what is hdmi | what is hdmi arc | what does hdmi cable do", "**HDMI** carries high-quality video and sound through one cable, from a console, laptop or streaming stick to a TV. **ARC/eARC** lets the TV send sound back down the cable to a soundbar."],
  ["what is screen burn in | can oled burn in | how to avoid burn in", "Burn-in is a faint ghost image left when the same thing, like a logo or game HUD, stays on screen for a very long time. Modern TVs have protection features, but varying what you watch and using a screen saver helps."],
  ["how does a projector work | what is a projector", "A projector shines a very bright light through or off a tiny image chip, then a lens enlarges and focuses that picture onto a wall or screen. It works best in a dark room."],
  ["what is a soundbar | do i need a soundbar", "TVs are thin, so their speakers are small. A **soundbar** is a long speaker that sits under the TV and gives clearer voices and fuller sound, often with an optional subwoofer."],
  ["what is a streaming stick | how do streaming devices work | streaming box", "A streaming stick or box plugs into a TV's HDMI port and connects to Wi-Fi, adding streaming apps to any TV. You control it with a remote or your phone."],
  ["how does a tv remote work | how does an infrared remote work | remote not working", "Most remotes flash an invisible **infrared** light in patterns, a different code for each button. The TV's sensor reads the code. Tip: point a phone camera at the remote and press a button; you may see the light blink. If it's dead, swap the batteries."],
  ["what is game mode on a tv | what is vrr | what is allm", "**Game mode** turns off extra picture processing to reduce input lag. **VRR** (variable refresh rate) matches the screen to the game's frame rate to stop tearing, and **ALLM** switches to game mode automatically when a console turns on."],
  ["what is response time | what does 1ms mean on a monitor | monitor response time", "Response time is how quickly pixels change color, measured in milliseconds. Lower is better for fast games, since slow pixels can cause blur or ghost trails behind moving objects."],
  ["is 144hz worth it | what is a 144hz monitor | high refresh rate monitor", "A **144Hz** monitor shows up to 144 images per second instead of 60, so motion feels much smoother and more responsive, especially in fast games. Your computer needs to produce high frame rates to get the full benefit."],
  ["what is screen tearing | what is g-sync | what is freesync", "Screen tearing is when parts of two frames show at once, making a jagged line. **Adaptive sync** technologies like G-Sync and FreeSync make the monitor wait for each new frame, which removes tearing."],
  ["ips vs va vs tn | what is an ips panel | monitor panel types", "**IPS** panels have the best colors and viewing angles. **VA** panels have deeper blacks and strong contrast. **TN** panels are cheap and fast but have weaker colors and angles."],
  ["what is an ultrawide monitor | what is aspect ratio | 16:9 vs 21:9", "Aspect ratio is a screen's width compared to its height. Most screens are **16:9**. **Ultrawide** monitors are about 21:9, giving extra side space for games, video editing or lots of windows."],
  ["what is 1440p | 1080p vs 1440p | what is qhd", "**1440p** (also called QHD) is 2560 by 1440 pixels, sharper than 1080p but easier to run than 4K. It's a popular middle ground for gaming monitors."],
  ["how do i set up two monitors | dual monitors | how to extend my display", "1. Plug the second monitor into a free video port (HDMI, DisplayPort or USB-C).\n2. On Windows, press **Win + P** and choose **Extend**.\n3. In display settings, drag the screens to match how they sit on your desk."],
  ["hdmi vs displayport | what is displayport", "Both carry video and audio. **DisplayPort** is common on PC monitors and graphics cards and handles high refresh rates well; **HDMI** is universal on TVs and consoles. Use whichever both devices support best."],
  ["what is ppi | what is pixel density | why do phone screens look so sharp", "PPI (pixels per inch) measures how tightly pixels are packed. Phones have high PPI because you hold them close, so you can't see individual pixels, while big TVs can have lower PPI because you sit farther away."],
  ["matte vs glossy screen | anti glare screen | which screen finish is better", "**Glossy** screens look vivid but reflect lights and windows. **Matte** (anti-glare) screens scatter reflections, so they're easier to use in bright rooms, though colors may look slightly softer."],
  ["what is blue light | do blue light glasses work | does blue light hurt your eyes", "Screens give off some blue light, but there's no strong evidence it damages eyes. It can make it harder to fall asleep late at night, so night-light modes help. Eye strain usually comes from staring too long, so take breaks."],
  ["what is the 20 20 20 rule | how to rest my eyes from screens | tired eyes from computer", "Every **20 minutes**, look at something about **20 feet** (6 meters) away for **20 seconds**. It gives your eye muscles a rest. Also remember to blink and keep the screen about an arm's length away."],
  ["mechanical vs membrane keyboard | what is a mechanical keyboard | are mechanical keyboards better", "A **mechanical** keyboard has a separate switch with a spring under each key, giving a crisp feel and long life. A **membrane** keyboard uses rubber domes over a sheet, making it quieter, lighter and cheaper. Which is better is personal taste."],
  ["red vs blue vs brown switches | what are keyboard switches | linear tactile clicky", "Mechanical switches come in three main feels:\n1. **Linear** (often red): smooth and quiet.\n2. **Tactile** (often brown): a little bump when the key activates.\n3. **Clicky** (often blue): a bump plus a loud click."],
  ["what is a 60 percent keyboard | keyboard sizes | what is a tkl keyboard", "A **full-size** keyboard has a number pad. **TKL** (tenkeyless) drops the number pad. A **75%** or **65%** board squeezes things tighter, and **60%** removes the arrow and function keys too, saving desk space."],
  ["what are keycaps | can you change keycaps | custom keycaps", "Keycaps are the plastic tops you press. On most mechanical keyboards you can pull them off with a keycap puller and swap in new colors or materials to customize the look and feel."],
  ["why is the keyboard not in abc order | what is qwerty | why is qwerty layout used", "QWERTY comes from early typewriters. Its layout spread out common letter pairs, which helped stop the metal arms from jamming. Everyone learned it, so it stuck around even on computers."],
  ["what is keyboard ghosting | what is n key rollover | anti ghosting", "**Ghosting** is when some key combinations don't register if you press several at once. **N-key rollover** means every key press is detected, no matter how many you hold, which gamers like."],
  ["how do i clean my keyboard | keyboard has crumbs | keys feel sticky", "1. Unplug it or turn it off.\n2. Turn it upside down and gently shake out crumbs.\n3. Use compressed air between the keys.\n4. Wipe keys with a cloth slightly dampened with isopropyl alcohol. Never pour liquid on it."],
  ["what is an ergonomic keyboard | what is a split keyboard | keyboard wrist pain", "Ergonomic keyboards are shaped or split so your wrists stay straighter and more relaxed. If typing hurts, also check your posture, keep wrists level, and take regular breaks."],
  ["how does a computer mouse work | what is an optical mouse | how does a mouse track movement", "An optical mouse has a tiny camera that takes thousands of pictures of the surface every second. By comparing them, it calculates which way and how far you moved and tells the computer."],
  ["what is mouse dpi | what dpi should i use | what is cpi", "**DPI** (or CPI) is how far the cursor moves for each inch you move the mouse. Higher DPI means faster cursor movement. Many people use 800 to 1600 and adjust sensitivity in the game or system settings."],
  ["what is polling rate | mouse polling rate | 1000hz mouse", "Polling rate is how often the mouse reports its position to the computer each second. 1000Hz means once every millisecond, which makes movement feel very responsive for gaming."],
  ["wired vs wireless mouse | is a wireless mouse laggy | best mouse for gaming", "Good modern wireless mice with a USB receiver are fast enough even for competitive gaming. Wired mice never need charging. Bluetooth mice are convenient but can have a little more delay."],
  ["what is a trackball | what is a vertical mouse | ergonomic mouse", "A **trackball** stays still while you roll a ball with your thumb or fingers. A **vertical mouse** lets you hold your hand in a handshake position. Both can be more comfortable for some people's wrists."],
  ["how does a touchpad work | what is a trackpad | trackpad gestures", "A laptop touchpad is a capacitive sensor, like a small touchscreen without a display. It tracks your fingers so you can move the cursor, tap to click and use gestures like two-finger scrolling."],
  ["inkjet vs laser printer | how does an inkjet printer work | how does a laser printer work", "**Inkjet** printers spray tiny drops of liquid ink, great for photos. **Laser** printers use a laser to draw the page on a drum with static electricity, pick up powder called **toner**, and melt it onto paper; they're fast and cheap per page for text."],
  ["why is printer ink so expensive | why does my printer run out of ink so fast", "Many companies sell printers cheaply and make their money on ink. Ink is also used for cleaning the print heads. For lots of printing, consider a laser printer or a printer with refillable ink tanks."],
  ["why are my prints streaky | printer printing faded | lines on printed pages", "1. Check ink or toner levels.\n2. Run the printer's **head cleaning** or nozzle check from its settings.\n3. Make sure you're using the right paper type setting.\n4. If it's a laser printer, gently shake the toner cartridge."],
  ["how do i fix a paper jam | paper stuck in printer | printer says paper jam", "1. Turn the printer off.\n2. Open the doors and trays shown in the manual.\n3. Gently pull the paper out in the direction it travels, with both hands so it doesn't tear.\n4. Check for torn bits, close everything, and turn it back on."],
  ["how does a scanner work | how do i scan a document | how to scan with my phone", "A scanner moves a light and sensor across the page to capture it as an image. No scanner? Many phone camera or notes apps have a **scan document** feature that straightens and sharpens the page for you."],
  ["what is dpi in printing | what dpi should i print photos at | print resolution", "In printing, DPI is how many dots of ink fit in an inch. For photos, around **300 DPI** looks sharp up close. Lower is fine for posters seen from far away."],
  ["my printer is offline | printer says offline | how do i fix printer offline", "1. Make sure the printer is on and shows no error.\n2. Check it's connected to the **same Wi-Fi** as your computer (or the USB cable is in).\n3. Restart the printer, then your computer.\n4. In printer settings, clear any stuck jobs and remove and re-add the printer if needed."],
  ["what is an external hard drive | external ssd | what is a portable drive", "An external drive is storage in a box that plugs in over USB. It's great for backups and moving big files. **External SSDs** are smaller and much faster; external hard drives give more space for less money."],
  ["what is a flash drive | what is a thumb drive | what is a memory stick", "A USB flash drive is a small stick of solid-state memory you plug into a USB port to carry files around. It has no moving parts, but it's easy to lose, so don't keep your only copy of important stuff on one."],
  ["what is an sd card | what is a microsd card | sd card speed classes", "SD and microSD cards are tiny memory cards used in cameras, consoles, drones and some phones. Speed markings like **U3** or **V30** tell you how fast they write, which matters for recording high-quality video."],
  ["why should i eject a usb drive | how do i safely eject a usb | is it ok to just pull out a usb", "Ejecting makes sure the computer has finished writing everything. Pulling the drive out mid-copy can corrupt files. Use **Eject** or **Safely remove**, then unplug once it says it's safe."],
  ["flash drive not showing up | usb drive not detected | computer doesnt see my usb drive", "1. Try a different USB port and a different computer.\n2. Check Disk Management (Windows) or Disk Utility (Mac) to see if it appears there.\n3. If it shows with no letter or as unformatted, it may need a drive letter or formatting (which erases it).\n4. If it's not seen anywhere, the drive may have failed."],
  ["what is a nas | network attached storage", "A **NAS** is a small box full of drives that connects to your home network, letting every device save, share and back up files to it, a bit like a personal cloud."],
  ["why does my 1tb drive show less space | drive smaller than advertised | where did my storage go", "Drive makers count 1 TB as 1,000,000,000,000 bytes, but computers count in powers of 1024, so it shows about **931 GB**. The system and preinstalled files also take some room."],
  ["how does cloud storage work | where are cloud files stored | is cloud storage safe", "Cloud storage saves your files on a company's servers in big data centers, so you can reach them from any device. It's generally safe if you use a **strong password and 2FA**, and the company keeps backup copies."],
  ["cloud storage vs external drive | what is the 3 2 1 backup rule | best way to back up", "Use both! The **3-2-1 rule**: keep 3 copies of important files, on 2 different kinds of storage, with 1 copy somewhere else, like the cloud. That way one accident can't wipe everything."],
  ["what does sync mean | what is file syncing | why do my files sync", "**Syncing** keeps the same files up to date on several devices. Change a file on your laptop and the cloud copy and your phone copy update too. That also means deleting it in one place can delete it everywhere."],
  ["does deleting from the cloud delete from my computer | what happens if i delete a synced file", "If a folder is syncing, deleting a file in one place usually deletes it everywhere. Most services keep deleted files in a **recycle bin** for a while, so you can often restore them."],
  ["how does a robot vacuum work | how does a robot vacuum find its way", "A robot vacuum uses bump sensors, cliff sensors to avoid stairs, and often lasers or cameras to map rooms. It plans a path, cleans, and returns to its dock to recharge when the battery runs low."],
  ["how does a smart doorbell work | video doorbell", "A smart doorbell has a camera, mic and speaker and connects to Wi-Fi. When someone rings or motion is detected, it sends an alert to your phone so you can see and talk to the visitor."],
  ["how does a smart thermostat work", "A smart thermostat connects to Wi-Fi so you can control heating and cooling from your phone. Many learn your schedule and adjust automatically to save energy when nobody's home."],
  ["how does face unlock work | how does face id work | is face unlock secure", "Phones with secure face unlock shine thousands of invisible dots on your face to build a **3D map**, which can't be fooled by a photo. Simpler versions just use the front camera image and are less secure."],
  ["how does a fingerprint scanner work | fingerprint sensor | in display fingerprint", "Fingerprint sensors capture the ridges of your finger using electrical charge, light, or sound waves. The phone compares the pattern to the one saved on the device; the fingerprint itself stays stored securely on your phone."],
  ["how does my phone know when to rotate | what is an accelerometer | what is a gyroscope", "An **accelerometer** senses gravity and movement, so it knows which way is down and rotates the screen. A **gyroscope** senses twisting and turning, which helps with games, VR and steady video."],
  ["how does a phone vibrate | what is haptics on a phone | vibration motor", "A tiny motor inside spins an off-balance weight or moves a mass back and forth, which shakes the phone. Fancy **haptic** motors can make precise taps that feel like clicks."],
  ["how does a walkie talkie work | how does a two way radio work", "A walkie-talkie turns your voice into radio waves on a set channel. Other radios tuned to that channel pick them up. Only one person can talk at a time, which is why you press a button to speak."],
  ["how does a radio work | am vs fm | how do radio signals carry music", "A radio station sends sound as radio waves. **AM** changes the wave's strength, which travels far but sounds fuzzier. **FM** changes the wave's frequency, which sounds clearer but has shorter range."],
  ["what is a surge protector | power strip vs surge protector | do i need a surge protector", "A power strip just adds outlets. A **surge protector** also absorbs sudden voltage spikes, like from lightning, that could damage electronics. It's a smart choice for computers, TVs and consoles."],
  ["what is a ups | uninterruptible power supply", "A **UPS** is a battery backup box. If the power goes out, it keeps your computer running long enough for you to save work and shut down safely."],
  ["what is a webcam | how does a webcam work | 720p vs 1080p webcam", "A webcam is a small camera for video calls and streaming. Higher resolution helps, but good **lighting** in front of you makes the biggest difference to how you look."],
  ["what is a capture card | how do streamers record console games", "A capture card takes the video signal from a console or camera and feeds it into a computer, so it can be recorded or streamed live."],
  ["how do security cameras work | what is a dashcam", "Security cameras record video to a memory card, a recorder box or the cloud, and many send alerts when they detect motion. A **dashcam** is a small camera in a car that records the road while driving."],
  ["how do led light bulbs work | why are led bulbs better", "LEDs make light when electricity passes through a special material, giving off very little heat. They use much less energy and last many times longer than old incandescent bulbs, which made light by heating a wire."],
  ["how does a calculator work | how do calculators do math", "A calculator turns your button presses into binary numbers, and tiny circuits of transistors perform the math using logic gates, then send the answer to the display, all in a fraction of a second."],
  ["what are smart glasses | how do smart glasses work", "Smart glasses look like normal glasses but include things like small speakers, a camera, or a tiny display that shows information in front of your eye. They usually connect to your phone."],
  ["how do wireless earbuds charge | why do earbuds have a case", "The case is a mini power bank. When you drop the earbuds in, metal contacts or magnets connect them and recharge them, and the case also keeps them safe and paired."],
  ["how many cpu cores do i need | what are cpu cores | what does quad core mean", "A **core** is like a separate worker inside the CPU. Quad-core means four. For school work and browsing, 4 cores is fine; for gaming, 6 to 8 is comfortable; video editing and 3D work benefit from even more."],
  ["what does ghz mean | what is clock speed | is higher ghz better", "**GHz** (gigahertz) is how many billion cycles a CPU core runs per second. Higher is faster within the same family of chips, but newer designs can do more work per cycle, so GHz alone doesn't tell the whole story."],
  ["cores vs threads | what are cpu threads | what is hyperthreading", "A **thread** is a stream of instructions. Some CPUs let each core juggle two threads at once (called hyper-threading or SMT), which helps keep the core busy and speeds up multitasking."],
  ["what is cpu cache | l1 l2 l3 cache | what is cache in a processor", "CPU cache is a tiny amount of super-fast memory right on the chip. It keeps data the CPU is likely to need next, so it doesn't wait for slower RAM. L1 is smallest and fastest, L3 is bigger and a bit slower."],
  ["what is a bottleneck | what is cpu bottleneck | what is bottlenecking", "A bottleneck is when one part holds the others back, like a powerful graphics card waiting on a slow CPU. A balanced PC has parts that are roughly matched for what you want to do."],
  ["integrated vs dedicated graphics | what is integrated graphics | do i need a graphics card", "**Integrated graphics** are built into the CPU and share system RAM; they're fine for school work, video and light games. A **dedicated** graphics card has its own chip and memory for demanding games, 3D and video work."],
  ["what is vram | how much vram do i need | graphics card memory", "**VRAM** is the graphics card's own memory for textures and images. More VRAM helps with high resolutions and detailed textures; running out can cause stutters, so lower texture settings if it does."],
  ["what else are gpus used for | why does ai use gpus | gpu for video editing", "Because GPUs do thousands of calculations at once, they're great for more than games: video editing and encoding, 3D rendering, scientific simulations and training AI models."],
  ["what is ray tracing | ray tracing in games | is ray tracing worth it", "Ray tracing simulates how light rays bounce around a scene, creating realistic reflections, shadows and lighting. It looks amazing but is demanding, so many games let you turn it on or off."],
  ["what is overclocking | is overclocking safe | how do you overclock", "Overclocking runs a CPU, GPU or RAM faster than its default speed for extra performance. It creates more heat and can cause crashes if pushed too far, so it needs good cooling and careful testing."],
  ["how much ram do i need for gaming | 8gb vs 16gb ram | is 32gb ram overkill", "For most gaming, **16 GB** is a comfortable amount. 8 GB works for lighter games and school work. 32 GB or more mostly helps with heavy video editing, 3D work or lots of programs open at once."],
  ["what is ddr ram | ddr4 vs ddr5 | does ram speed matter", "DDR is the type of RAM; each new generation (DDR4, DDR5) is faster and more efficient but needs a matching motherboard. Faster RAM gives a small boost, while having **enough** RAM matters more."],
  ["what is dual channel ram | why install ram in pairs | ram slots", "Using two matching sticks of RAM in the right slots lets the CPU talk to both at once (**dual channel**), which increases speed. Check the motherboard manual for which slots to use."],
  ["how do i upgrade ram | can i add more ram to my laptop | how to install ram", "1. Check what RAM type and maximum your computer supports.\n2. Turn it off, unplug it, and touch metal to discharge static.\n3. Open the panel, push the slot clips aside, and press the new stick in until it clicks.\nNote: many thin laptops have RAM soldered on and can't be upgraded."],
  ["what is nvme | nvme vs sata ssd | what is m.2", "**M.2** is the small stick shape. **NVMe** SSDs plug into M.2 slots and talk over super-fast PCIe lanes, making them several times quicker than older **SATA** SSDs, which are still much faster than hard drives."],
  ["do ssds wear out | how long does an ssd last | ssd lifespan", "SSD memory cells can only be rewritten so many times, but modern SSDs are built to handle years of normal use. Most people replace their computer before the SSD wears out. Keep backups anyway."],
  ["how does a hard drive work | why are hard drives slower | what is a spinning disk", "A hard drive stores data magnetically on spinning metal platters, and an arm moves a tiny head to read and write. Moving parts take time, so it's much slower than an SSD and can be damaged if dropped while running."],
  ["how much storage do i need | 256gb vs 512gb | how many gb do i need", "For a phone or school laptop that mostly uses the cloud, 128 to 256 GB can work. If you install games, take lots of video or edit photos, aim for **512 GB to 1 TB** or more. Modern games alone can each take dozens of GB."],
  ["what is a chipset | motherboard sizes | atx vs micro atx", "The **chipset** controls how the motherboard's parts communicate and which features it supports. Common sizes are **ATX** (full size), **Micro-ATX** (smaller) and **Mini-ITX** (tiny). Your case must fit the size you choose."],
  ["what is a cpu socket | will this cpu fit my motherboard | cpu compatibility", "The socket is the slot where the CPU sits. A CPU only works in a motherboard with the matching socket and chipset, so check compatibility lists from the motherboard maker before buying."],
  ["how many watts power supply do i need | psu wattage | what is 80 plus", "Add up what your parts use (the graphics card and CPU use the most) and leave some headroom; online PSU calculators help. **80 Plus** ratings (Bronze, Gold and so on) show how efficiently it converts power and wastes less as heat."],
  ["what is a modular power supply | modular vs non modular psu", "A **modular** power supply lets you plug in only the cables you need, keeping the inside of the PC tidier and airflow better. Non-modular ones have all cables permanently attached."],
  ["why does a computer have fans | how does a computer stay cool | computer cooling", "Chips turn electricity into heat as they work. Fans pull cool air in and push hot air out, and **heat sinks** with metal fins spread the heat so it can be carried away. Too much heat makes computers slow down or shut off."],
  ["air cooling vs liquid cooling | what is an aio cooler | water cooling a pc", "**Air coolers** use a metal heat sink and fan; simple and reliable. **Liquid coolers** (AIOs) pump liquid from the CPU to a radiator with fans, which can cool better and look neat but have more parts."],
  ["what is thermal paste | do i need thermal paste | how much thermal paste", "Thermal paste fills tiny gaps between the CPU and cooler so heat transfers well. Many coolers come with it pre-applied. If not, a pea-sized dot in the middle is usually enough."],
  ["what is thermal throttling | why does my computer slow down when hot | cpu too hot", "When a chip gets too hot, it automatically slows itself down to cool off, which is **thermal throttling**. Clean dusty vents, keep laptops on hard surfaces, and make sure fans are working."],
  ["what is a heat sink | how does a heatsink work", "A heat sink is a block of metal, often with thin fins, that sits on a hot chip. It soaks up heat and spreads it over a big surface so air flowing past can carry it away."],
  ["how do i build a pc | steps to build a computer | what parts do i need for a pc", "Parts: CPU, motherboard, RAM, storage, power supply, case, cooler, and usually a graphics card. Basic steps:\n1. Put the CPU, cooler, RAM and M.2 SSD on the motherboard.\n2. Mount the motherboard in the case.\n3. Install the power supply and graphics card.\n4. Connect all cables, then install an operating system. Building with an experienced adult helps a lot."],
  ["is building a pc hard | should i build or buy a pc | is it cheaper to build a pc", "Building a PC is a bit like advanced LEGO with instructions: very doable with patience and video guides. It lets you choose every part and upgrade later. Buying prebuilt saves time and comes with one warranty."],
  ["do i need an anti static wrist strap | static electricity when building a pc", "Static shocks can damage parts. A wrist strap is nice but not required: work on a hard floor, avoid carpet, and touch the metal case often to discharge yourself."],
  ["new pc wont turn on | built pc no display | computer not posting", "1. Check the power supply switch is on and all power cables (24-pin and CPU 8-pin) are firmly in.\n2. Plug the monitor into the **graphics card**, not the motherboard.\n3. Reseat the RAM until it clicks.\n4. Look for debug lights on the motherboard and check the manual."],
  ["what is post | what does post mean on a computer", "**POST** (power-on self-test) is a quick check the computer runs when it turns on, making sure the CPU, memory and other key parts work before loading the operating system. Beeps or lights can signal a problem."],
  ["what is a pc case | what is a mid tower | why does case airflow matter", "The case holds and protects the parts. **Mid-towers** are the most common size. Good airflow, with fans pulling air in the front and pushing it out the back and top, keeps everything cool."],
  ["what is a file system | what is ntfs | fat32 vs exfat", "A file system is how a drive organizes files. **NTFS** is used by Windows drives, **APFS** by Macs, and **ext4** often by Linux. **exFAT** and **FAT32** work on almost every device, which makes them handy for USB sticks."],
  ["file too large for destination | why cant i copy a big file to my usb | fat32 4gb limit", "FAT32 can't hold a single file bigger than **4 GB**. To fix it, back up the drive and reformat it as **exFAT** (or NTFS for Windows-only use). Formatting erases the drive."],
  ["what does format a drive mean | how do i format a usb | what is formatting", "Formatting prepares a drive with a file system and **erases** everything on it. On Windows, right-click the drive in File Explorer and choose Format; on Mac, use Disk Utility. Back up first!"],
  ["what is a partition | what is partitioning a drive", "A partition splits one physical drive into separate sections that act like different drives. People use them to keep the system separate from files or to install two operating systems."],
  ["what is defragmenting | should i defrag my ssd | what is fragmentation", "On hard drives, file pieces can end up scattered, slowing things down; **defragmenting** puts them back together. SSDs don't need it, and Windows automatically optimizes them in a different way instead."],
  ["how do i update drivers | why update drivers | where do i get drivers", "Most drivers update automatically through Windows Update. For graphics cards, get drivers from the card maker's official app or website. Update when something isn't working right or a new game needs it."],
  ["bios vs uefi | what is bios | what is uefi", "The **BIOS** is the firmware that starts your computer before the operating system loads. **UEFI** is its modern replacement, with a graphical menu, mouse support, faster startup and features like Secure Boot. People still often call it the BIOS."],
  ["how do i get into the bios | bios key | how to enter bios settings", "Restart and tap the setup key right as the computer starts, often **Delete**, **F2**, **F10** or **F12** depending on the maker. On Windows you can also go to Settings → Recovery → Advanced startup → UEFI Firmware Settings."],
  ["what is secure boot | what is tpm | why does windows need tpm", "**Secure Boot** makes sure only trusted software loads at startup, blocking some sneaky malware. A **TPM** is a security chip that safely stores encryption keys. Newer versions of Windows require both."],
  ["what is booting | what is boot order | what does boot mean", "**Booting** is the startup process, when firmware loads the operating system. **Boot order** is the list of drives the computer checks for an operating system, like the SSD first, then a USB stick."],
  ["what is firmware | firmware vs software | what is a firmware update", "**Firmware** is software built into a device's hardware that controls how it works, like in routers, controllers and TVs. Firmware updates fix bugs and add features, so install them, and don't unplug during one."],
  ["which operating system should i use | compare windows macos and linux | windows or mac or linux", "All are good, they just differ:\n1. **Windows**: the most software and games, many hardware choices.\n2. **macOS**: only on Macs, polished and popular for creative work.\n3. **Linux**: free and very customizable, popular with programmers.\n4. **ChromeOS**: simple and web-based, common in schools."],
  ["what is macos | what is a mac | what is the mac operating system", "**macOS** is Apple's operating system for Mac computers. It's known for a clean design, working smoothly with iPhones and iPads, and popular creative apps."],
  ["what is chromeos | what is a chromebook | can a chromebook run windows apps", "A **Chromebook** runs ChromeOS, which is built around the Chrome browser and web apps. It starts fast and is easy to manage. It can run Android apps and sometimes Linux apps, but not regular Windows programs."],
  ["ios vs android | iphone vs android | what is the difference between iphone and android", "**iOS** runs only on iPhones and offers a very consistent experience. **Android** runs on phones from many different makers, with more variety in prices and customization. Both have huge app stores and strong security if kept updated."],
  ["what is android | who makes android | is android open source", "Android is a mobile operating system developed by Google and based on Linux. Its core is open source, and many manufacturers put it on their phones, tablets and TVs."],
  ["what is ios | what is ipados | what does ios stand for", "iOS is Apple's operating system for iPhone. **iPadOS** is a version made for iPad, with extras for bigger screens like multitasking. The name originally came from iPhone OS."],
  ["what is a linux distro | what is a linux distribution | popular linux distros", "A **distro** is a complete operating system built around the Linux kernel, bundled with apps and a desktop. Popular ones include Ubuntu, Linux Mint, Fedora, Debian and Arch, each with its own style."],
  ["what is dual boot | can i run linux and windows on one computer | how to dual boot", "Dual booting means installing two operating systems on one computer and choosing which to start each time it turns on. Another option is a **virtual machine**, which runs one OS inside a window of another."],
  ["32 bit vs 64 bit | what is a 64 bit operating system | what does x64 mean", "A **64-bit** system can handle far more memory and bigger numbers than a 32-bit one. Almost all modern computers and phones are 64-bit, and x64 is another name for it on PCs."],
  ["is linux free | why is linux free | what is free software", "Most Linux distros are free to download and use because they're open source, built and shared by communities and companies around the world. Anyone can study, change and share the code."],
  ["why is the main drive called c | what happened to drive a and b | what is the c drive", "Early PCs used **A:** and **B:** for floppy disk drives. When hard drives arrived, they got the next letter, **C:**, and the tradition stuck."],
  ["what is a file path | how do file paths work | what is a directory", "A file path is a file's address, showing the folders you go through to reach it, like C:\\Users\\Name\\Documents\\essay.docx on Windows or /home/name/essay.txt on Mac and Linux."],
  ["what is the windows registry | what is the registry", "The **registry** is a big database where Windows and apps store their settings. Editing it by hand can break things, so only change it if you're following trusted instructions and have a backup."],
  ["what is safe mode | how do i start in safe mode | why use safe mode", "Safe mode starts the computer with only the essential drivers and programs. It helps you fix problems like a bad driver or malware. On Windows, hold **Shift** while clicking Restart, then choose Troubleshoot → Advanced options → Startup Settings."],
  ["what is a blue screen | what is bsod | blue screen of death", "A **blue screen** appears when Windows hits a serious error it can't recover from, often caused by a bad driver, faulty hardware or overheating. Restart; if it keeps happening, note the stop code and update drivers or check hardware."],
  ["what is system restore | what is a restore point | undo a bad update", "**System Restore** rolls Windows settings, drivers and programs back to an earlier restore point, without deleting your personal files. It's handy when a new driver or update causes trouble."],
  ["laptop or desktop which is better | laptop vs desktop | should i get a laptop or a desktop", "A **laptop** is portable with a built-in screen and battery. A **desktop** gives more power for the money, is easier to upgrade and keeps cooler, but stays in one place."],
  ["tablet vs laptop | is a tablet good for school | should i get a tablet", "Tablets are light, great for reading, drawing and videos, and have long battery life. Laptops are better for typing long essays, multitasking and running full programs. Adding a keyboard to a tablet narrows the gap."],
  ["how do i read computer specs | what specs matter | what should i look for in a laptop", "Key specs:\n1. **CPU**: the brain; more cores and newer generations are faster.\n2. **RAM**: 8 GB minimum, 16 GB is comfy.\n3. **Storage**: an SSD, 256 GB or more.\n4. **Screen** and **battery life**.\n5. **GPU** if you'll play games."],
  ["how do i check my pc specs | what computer do i have | how to see my ram and cpu", "On Windows, open **Settings → System → About** to see your CPU and RAM, or open **Task Manager → Performance**. On a Mac, click the Apple menu and choose **About This Mac**."],
  ["what is an soc | what is a system on a chip | what is an apu", "A **system on a chip** puts the CPU, graphics, memory controller and more onto one chip. Phones, tablets and many laptops use them because they save space and power. An **APU** is a similar idea for PCs."],
  ["arm vs x86 | what is an arm processor | what is x86", "**x86** chips have powered most PCs for decades. **ARM** chips are designed to be very power-efficient and run nearly all phones, and now many laptops too. Apps sometimes need versions made for each type."],
  ["what is a benchmark | what is a cpu benchmark | how do you compare computer speed", "A benchmark is a standard test that measures how fast a part or computer performs, so you can compare different models fairly. Real-world performance can still vary by task."],
  ["what does nm mean for chips | what is a 3nm chip | what is a chip process node", "Chip makers describe their manufacturing with numbers in nanometers. Smaller numbers generally mean newer techniques that fit more transistors in less space and use less power, though the number is more a label than an exact measurement."],
  ["what is a virtual machine used for | why use a virtual machine | how do virtual machines work", "A virtual machine is a pretend computer running inside a real one, with its own operating system. It's great for testing software or trying another OS safely, since whatever happens inside stays sealed off."],
  ["what is a pcie slot | what is pcie | what plugs into pcie", "**PCIe** slots on a motherboard are high-speed connectors for expansion cards like graphics cards, Wi-Fi cards and capture cards. NVMe SSDs use PCIe lanes too."],
  ["what is a cmos battery | why does my computer forget the time", "A small coin battery on the motherboard keeps the clock and some firmware settings saved while the computer is unplugged. If the time keeps resetting, that battery may need replacing."],
  ["what is an operating system update vs upgrade | update vs upgrade", "An **update** is a smaller fix or improvement to the version you have, like security patches. An **upgrade** moves you to a whole new version with bigger changes."],
  ["what is a command line used for | why do programmers use the terminal | gui vs command line", "A **GUI** lets you click buttons and icons. A **command line** lets you type instructions, which is fast for repeating tasks, automating things and controlling servers that have no screen."],
  ["what is task scheduler | what are background processes | why are so many programs running", "Many programs run quietly in the background to check for updates, sync files or handle hardware. You can see them in Task Manager and stop unneeded ones from starting automatically in **Startup apps**."],
  ["how do i stop apps from opening at startup | startup programs | computer slow to boot", "On Windows, open **Task Manager → Startup apps** and disable programs you don't need right away. On a Mac, go to **System Settings → General → Login Items**. Fewer startup apps means a faster boot."],
  ["what is a screen saver for | do i need a screen saver", "Screen savers were invented to stop old screens from burning in images. Today it's better to just let the display turn off after a few minutes to save energy."],
  ["ipv4 vs ipv6 | why do we need ipv6 | what is ipv6", "**IPv4** addresses look like 192.0.2.1 and allow about 4 billion addresses, which isn't enough for every device on Earth. **IPv6** addresses are much longer, allowing a practically endless number."],
  ["public vs private ip address | what is 192.168 | what is a local ip address", "Your router has one **public IP** that the internet sees. Inside your home, it gives each device a **private IP**, often starting with 192.168, and keeps track of which data goes to which device."],
  ["can someone find me from my ip address | what does my ip address reveal | is it bad if someone has my ip", "An IP address usually reveals your internet provider and a rough area, like a city, not your home address or name. Still, it's best not to share it, and tell a trusted adult if someone online threatens you with it."],
  ["what is an isp | what is an internet service provider | who provides my internet", "An **ISP** is the company that connects your home or phone to the internet, through cable, fiber, phone lines, mobile networks or satellite. You pay them for a plan with a certain speed."],
  ["fiber vs cable vs dsl | what is fiber internet | types of internet connections", "**Fiber** sends data as light through glass strands; it's the fastest and most reliable. **Cable** uses TV coaxial lines. **DSL** uses phone lines and is slower. **Mobile** and **satellite** internet work where cables don't reach."],
  ["how does satellite internet work | what is satellite internet", "A dish on your home talks to satellites in space, which connect to ground stations linked to the internet. Newer low-orbit satellites are closer to Earth, which gives lower delay than older high-orbit ones."],
  ["bandwidth vs latency | difference between speed and ping | is ping the same as speed", "**Bandwidth** is how much data can flow per second, like the width of a pipe. **Latency** (ping) is how long one piece of data takes to make the trip. Streaming cares most about bandwidth; online gaming cares most about latency."],
  ["what is a good ping | is 100 ping good | what ping is good for gaming", "Under **20 ms** is excellent, 20 to 50 ms is great, 50 to 100 ms is okay for most games, and over 100 ms you'll start noticing lag. Wired connections and nearby servers give the lowest ping."],
  ["what is jitter | what is packet loss | why does my call keep cutting out", "**Jitter** is when ping jumps up and down unevenly. **Packet loss** is when some data never arrives. Both cause choppy calls and rubber-banding in games. A wired connection and fewer busy devices can help."],
  ["upload vs download speed | why is upload slower | what is upload speed", "**Download** is data coming to you, like videos. **Upload** is data you send, like video calls or posting videos. Many home plans give much more download than upload because most people download more."],
  ["what internet speed do i need | how fast should my internet be | is 100 mbps good", "A rough guide: HD streaming needs about 5 to 10 Mbps per stream, 4K about 25 Mbps, and video calls a few Mbps up and down. A busy household with gamers and streamers is happiest with 100 Mbps or more."],
  ["how do i test my internet speed | internet speed test | check my wifi speed", "Search for an internet **speed test** and run it in your browser. It measures download, upload and ping. For the real speed coming into your home, test on a device plugged in with an Ethernet cable."],
  ["megabits vs megabytes | mbps vs mb/s | why is my download slower than my internet speed", "Internet speed is in **megabits** (Mb), but file sizes are in **megabytes** (MB). One byte is 8 bits, so a 100 Mbps connection downloads at most about 12.5 MB per second."],
  ["what is a data cap | what is unlimited data | what is throttling", "A **data cap** limits how much you can use each month. Unlimited plans may still **throttle** (slow down) your speed after a certain amount, so read the plan's details."],
  ["what is a dns server | why change dns | what is 8.8.8.8", "A DNS server is the internet's phone book: it turns names like example.com into IP addresses. Your ISP provides one, but you can switch to public ones (like 8.8.8.8 or 1.1.1.1) that are sometimes faster or offer filtering."],
  ["what is a packet | how is data sent over the internet | how does data travel online", "Data is chopped into small chunks called **packets**, each labeled with where it's going. They may take different routes across the internet and get put back together in the right order when they arrive."],
  ["what is tcp ip | what is a protocol | what is an internet protocol", "A **protocol** is a set of rules for how computers talk. **IP** handles addressing and delivering packets, and **TCP** makes sure they all arrive and are in the right order. Together they're the internet's foundation."],
  ["what is a port number | what is port 443 | what are network ports", "A port number tells a computer which program should get incoming data, like apartment numbers in a building. Websites use port 80 for HTTP and **443** for secure HTTPS."],
  ["what is a cdn | content delivery network", "A **CDN** keeps copies of websites, images and videos on servers all over the world, so you load them from one nearby. That makes pages and streams load faster."],
  ["what is a proxy server | proxy vs vpn", "A **proxy** passes your web requests through another server, hiding your IP from the sites you visit. A **VPN** does that for all your device's traffic and also **encrypts** it. Schools often use proxies to filter content."],
  ["does a vpn make me anonymous | should i use a vpn | is a vpn safe", "A VPN hides your traffic from your local network and your IP from websites, which is useful on public Wi-Fi. It does **not** make you fully anonymous: the VPN company can see your traffic, and accounts you sign into still know it's you. Choose a trustworthy one."],
  ["what is tor | what is the tor browser | what is onion routing", "**Tor** is free software that bounces your connection through several volunteer computers, each adding a layer of encryption like an onion. It makes it hard to trace who visited a site, and is used by journalists and people protecting their privacy. It's slower than normal browsing."],
  ["what is the dark web | dark web vs deep web | what is the deep web", "The **deep web** is anything not found by search engines, like your email inbox or school portal, which is most of the web. The **dark web** is a small part only reachable with special software like Tor. It has some legitimate uses, but also scams and illegal stuff, so it's best avoided."],
  ["what are third party cookies | why do websites ask about cookies | what is a cookie banner", "**First-party** cookies come from the site you're on and do things like keep you logged in. **Third-party** cookies come from other companies, often advertisers, tracking you across sites. Cookie banners ask your permission; choosing **reject** or **necessary only** limits tracking."],
  ["why do ads follow me around | why do i see ads for things i searched | how does online tracking work", "Websites and apps share data with ad companies using cookies and trackers, building a profile of your interests to show targeted ads. You can limit it by rejecting cookies, using privacy settings and turning off ad personalization."],
  ["does incognito mode hide me | is incognito private | can my school see incognito", "Incognito mode only stops your browser from saving history, cookies and form data on **your device**. Your school or home network, internet provider and the websites you visit can still see what you do."],
  ["what is seo | how do search engines rank pages | why are some results first", "Search engines rank pages using many signals: how well the page matches your words, how useful and trusted it is, and how many other sites link to it. **SEO** (search engine optimization) is the work of making pages easier to find."],
  ["what is a web crawler | how does a search engine find websites | what is a search index", "Search engines use programs called **crawlers** that follow links from page to page, reading content and storing it in a giant **index**. When you search, they look through that index rather than the whole web."],
  ["what are sponsored search results | are top search results always right | what does ad mean in search results", "Results marked **Sponsored** or **Ad** are paid placements. And even top results can be wrong or one-sided, so check a few different trustworthy sources."],
  ["how do emails travel | what is smtp | how does an email get delivered", "When you hit send, your email goes to your provider's mail server, which uses **SMTP** to pass it to the recipient's mail server. It waits there until their app downloads it, often within seconds."],
  ["cc vs bcc | what is cc in email | what does bcc mean", "**CC** (carbon copy) sends a copy to others, and everyone can see who got it. **BCC** (blind carbon copy) sends copies secretly, so recipients can't see each other's addresses, which is polite for big group emails."],
  ["why wont my email attachment send | email attachment too big | attachment size limit", "Most email services limit attachments to around 20 to 25 MB. For bigger files, upload them to cloud storage and send a **share link** instead, or zip them to make them smaller."],
  ["how do spam filters work | why did my email go to spam | why are emails going to junk", "Spam filters look at things like suspicious words, links, the sender's reputation, and whether the email is properly verified. Real emails sometimes get caught, so check your spam folder and mark them **Not spam**."],
  ["how do i stop spam emails | how to unsubscribe safely | too many emails", "For real companies, use the **unsubscribe** link at the bottom. For obvious scams, don't click anything; just mark them as spam or junk so the filter learns. Avoid giving your email to random sites."],
  ["why does my video keep buffering | what is buffering | why does streaming pause", "Buffering means the video is downloading slower than it plays, so it pauses to catch up. Try moving closer to the router, pausing other downloads, lowering the video quality, or restarting your router."],
  ["why does video quality change while streaming | what is adaptive streaming | auto quality", "Streaming services use **adaptive streaming**: they keep checking your connection and switch to higher or lower quality on the fly, so the video keeps playing instead of stopping to buffer."],
  ["how much data does streaming use | how much data does an hour of video use | does streaming use a lot of data", "Roughly: standard definition uses about 1 GB per hour, HD about 3 GB, and 4K around 7 GB or more. Music uses much less, roughly 50 to 150 MB per hour. Download over Wi-Fi to save mobile data."],
  ["streaming vs downloading | what is the difference between streaming and downloading", "**Streaming** plays the content as it arrives and doesn't keep a full copy. **Downloading** saves the whole file to your device first, so you can watch it later without internet."],
  ["what is a podcast | how do podcasts work", "A podcast is an audio (sometimes video) show split into episodes that you can stream or download from podcast apps. You can subscribe to get new episodes automatically."],
  ["is the internet down or is it me | how do i know if a website is down | internet outage", "1. Try a few other websites.\n2. Try another device or switch from Wi-Fi to mobile data.\n3. If only one site fails, it may be down for everyone; search to check.\n4. If nothing works, restart your router and check your ISP's outage page on your phone."],
  ["what is web hosting | where are websites stored | what is a web host", "A website's files live on a **server** owned by a hosting company, which keeps it online 24/7. When you visit, your browser downloads the pages from that server."],
  ["what is a domain extension | what is .com and .org | what is a tld", "The ending of a web address is the **top-level domain**. **.com** started for companies, **.org** for organizations, **.edu** for colleges, and there are country ones like .uk or .ca. Anyone can register many of them, so the ending alone doesn't prove a site is trustworthy."],
  ["what is a 404 error | what does page not found mean | error 404", "A **404** means the server is working but can't find that page; it may have moved or the link has a typo. Check the address or go to the site's home page and search."],
  ["what do http error codes mean | what is a 500 error | what is a 403 error", "Codes starting with **4** mean a problem with the request, like 404 (not found) or 403 (forbidden). Codes starting with **5**, like 500 or 503, mean the website's server has a problem; try again later."],
  ["what is a captcha | why do websites ask if im a robot | why do i have to click on traffic lights", "A **CAPTCHA** is a little test that's easy for people but hard for automated bots, which helps stop spam and fake accounts. Many now just watch how you move your mouse instead."],
  ["what is a browser extension | are extensions safe | what are add ons", "Extensions add features to your browser, like ad blockers or dictionaries. Only install them from the official store, check reviews, and be careful with ones that ask to **read all your data on all websites**."],
  ["what is a pop up blocker | why do pop ups appear | how do i stop pop ups", "Browsers block most pop-up windows automatically. If you see lots of pop-ups, especially scary ones, close the tab, don't click inside, and check for sketchy extensions or notification permissions you allowed."],
  ["why does a website want to send me notifications | stop website notifications | browser notifications spam", "Some sites ask to send notifications, and scammy ones use them to spam ads. Click **Block** when asked. To remove ones you allowed, open the browser's settings → site permissions → notifications."],
  ["should i save passwords in my browser | is browser autofill safe | what is autofill", "Built-in browser password managers are a decent choice and much safer than reusing one password. Lock your device with a PIN, use 2FA on your account, and never save passwords on a shared or public computer."],
  ["what is an ad blocker | do ad blockers work", "An ad blocker is a browser extension that hides ads and many trackers, making pages cleaner and sometimes faster. Some sites rely on ads to stay free, so some people allow ads on sites they like."],
  ["what is a mac address | mac address vs ip address", "A **MAC address** is a hardware ID built into each network adapter, used on your local network. An **IP address** is used to find you across the wider internet and can change."],
  ["what is a network switch | switch vs router | what is a hub", "A **switch** connects several wired devices on the same local network and sends data only to the right one. A **router** connects your network to the internet. An old **hub** sent data to every device, which was less efficient."],
  ["what is an access point | what is a wireless access point", "An **access point** adds Wi-Fi to a wired network. It's often built into home routers, but businesses and schools use many separate ones on ceilings to cover big buildings."],
  ["cat5e vs cat6 | ethernet cable types | which ethernet cable should i buy", "Ethernet cables come in categories. **Cat5e** handles 1 Gbps, which is fine for most homes. **Cat6** and above support faster speeds over shorter runs. Any of them is usually more stable than Wi-Fi."],
  ["what is a powerline adapter | internet through electrical outlets", "Powerline adapters send your network through your home's electrical wiring. Plug one near the router and one in a far room to get a wired connection without running long cables."],
  ["why is my internet slow at night | internet slow at certain times | peak hours internet", "In the evening, lots of people in your area stream and game at once, which can crowd your provider's network. Also check whether other people at home are downloading big files at the same time."],
  ["what is net neutrality", "**Net neutrality** is the idea that internet providers should treat all online traffic equally, not speeding up or slowing down certain websites or apps. Different countries have different rules about it."],
  ["what is a firewall on a router | does my router have a firewall | router security tips", "Most home routers block unwanted incoming connections by default. To keep it secure: change the admin password, use WPA2 or WPA3, keep its firmware updated and turn off features you don't use."],
  ["what is a guest network | why use a guest wifi network", "A **guest network** is a separate Wi-Fi network for visitors or smart gadgets. It lets them get online without accessing your computers and files, which adds a layer of safety."],
  ["what is the cloud made of | where is the internet physically | how do internet cables cross the ocean", "The internet is very physical: huge data centers full of servers, plus cables, including fiber optic cables laid along the **ocean floor** between continents, carrying most international traffic."],
  ["what is a server farm | how big are data centers", "A data center or server farm is a giant building filled with rows of computers that store websites, videos and cloud files. They need tons of electricity and cooling to keep running 24/7."],
  ["how does video calling work | how do video calls work | why is my video call blurry", "Your camera and mic capture you, the app compresses the video and sends it as packets to the other person in real time. If your connection gets weaker, the app lowers the quality to keep the call going, so it gets blurry."],
  ["what is a qr code login | what is a magic link | passwordless login", "Some sites let you log in without typing a password, by scanning a QR code with an app that's already signed in, or by clicking a one-time **magic link** sent to your email. Only use links you requested yourself."],
  ["how does online shopping work | how do websites take payments", "When you buy online, your payment details are sent encrypted to a payment processor that checks with your bank. Kids should always ask a parent before buying anything, and only shop on sites with HTTPS from trusted stores."],
  ["what is an rss feed | what is rss", "**RSS** is a way for websites to publish a list of their newest posts. A feed reader app collects them all in one place, so you can follow many sites without visiting each one."],
  ["what is a web app | web app vs app | what is a pwa", "A **web app** runs inside your browser, with no install needed. A **PWA** (progressive web app) is a web app you can install like a normal app with its own icon. This Windows 12 sim itself is a web app!"],
  ["how do websites remember me | why am i still logged in | what is a session", "When you log in, the website gives your browser a **session cookie**, like a temporary ID badge. Each time you visit, your browser shows it, so you stay logged in until you log out or it expires."],
  ["what is encryption in transit | what is end to end encryption | what does end to end encrypted mean", "**End-to-end encryption** means messages are scrambled on your device and only unscrambled on the recipient's, so even the app company can't read them. Normal HTTPS protects data while traveling between you and the server."],
  ["what is a url shortener | are short links safe | what is a short link", "URL shorteners turn long web addresses into tiny ones. They're handy, but they hide the real destination, so be careful clicking short links from strangers. Some link-preview tools show where they lead."],
  ["what is ping command | how do i ping a website | how to use ping", "On a computer terminal, type **ping** followed by a site name, like ping example.com. It sends small test messages and shows how many milliseconds each round trip takes, which helps test your connection."],
  ["what is a network outage on my phone | what is a dead zone | why does my call drop in the same spot", "A **dead zone** is an area with weak or no signal, often due to hills, thick walls or distance from towers. Wi-Fi calling or a signal booster can help at home."],
  ["what is mobile data vs wifi | does wifi use my data | why use wifi instead of data", "**Wi-Fi** uses your home or school internet. **Mobile data** comes from your phone plan and is often limited. Using Wi-Fi when you can saves your data allowance, especially for videos and updates."],
  ["what is a modem router combo | what is a gateway | do i need a separate modem", "Many providers give you a **gateway**, which combines the modem (connects to the internet line) and router (creates your home network and Wi-Fi) in one box. Separate units can offer more control and upgrades."],
  ["what does restarting the router do | why does unplugging the router fix internet | how to reboot a router", "Restarting clears glitches and memory, reconnects to your provider and picks a fresh Wi-Fi channel. Unplug it, wait about 30 seconds, plug it back in and give it a few minutes to fully start."],
  ["what does wps button do | what is wps on a router", "**WPS** lets you connect a device by pushing a button on the router instead of typing the password. It's convenient but has had security weaknesses, so many people leave it turned off."],
  ["my phone wont turn on | phone black screen wont turn on | phone dead and wont start", "1. Plug it into a charger you know works and wait **15 to 30 minutes**.\n2. Try a different cable and outlet.\n3. Do a **force restart** (hold power with a volume button; check your phone's steps).\n4. Look for damage or swelling. If nothing works, take it to a repair shop."],
  ["my computer wont turn on | pc wont start | laptop wont power on", "1. Check the power cable, outlet and power strip switch.\n2. For a laptop, plug in the charger and wait a few minutes; check for a charging light.\n3. Hold the power button for 15 to 30 seconds, then try again.\n4. Unplug extras like USB drives.\n5. If fans spin but the screen is black, try another monitor or cable."],
  ["my laptop is overheating | laptop fan is really loud | computer getting too hot", "1. Use it on a hard, flat surface, not a bed or blanket.\n2. Make sure vents aren't blocked and clean out dust gently.\n3. Close heavy programs and extra browser tabs.\n4. Check Task Manager for apps using lots of CPU.\n5. A laptop cooling pad can help. If it shuts off from heat, get it checked."],
  ["my phone is overheating | phone getting hot | why is my phone so hot", "1. Take it out of direct sunlight and hot cars.\n2. Remove the case and stop charging for a while.\n3. Close games and camera apps that work it hard.\n4. Turn off what you aren't using, like hotspot or GPS.\n5. Let it cool naturally; never put it in a fridge or freezer."],
  ["my phone battery dies fast | phone battery drains quickly | why is my phone battery dying so fast", "1. Check **Settings → Battery** to see which apps use the most.\n2. Lower screen brightness and screen timeout.\n3. Turn on battery saver or low power mode.\n4. Limit background refresh and location for apps that don't need it.\n5. Check battery health; an old battery may need replacing."],
  ["my phone is slow | phone is lagging | how do i speed up my phone", "1. Restart the phone.\n2. Free up storage; phones slow down when nearly full.\n3. Update the system and apps.\n4. Close or uninstall apps you don't use.\n5. Turn off live wallpapers and heavy widgets."],
  ["my phone has no sound | phone speaker not working | i cant hear my phone", "1. Check the volume and that silent or do-not-disturb mode is off.\n2. Make sure it isn't connected to Bluetooth headphones or a speaker.\n3. Gently clean the speaker grille with a soft brush.\n4. Restart the phone.\n5. Test with a different app to see if it's just one app."],
  ["my phone wont connect to wifi | phone wifi not working | phone keeps disconnecting from wifi", "1. Turn Wi-Fi off and on, or toggle airplane mode.\n2. **Forget** the network and reconnect with the password.\n3. Restart the phone and the router.\n4. Check that other devices can connect.\n5. Try resetting network settings as a last resort (you'll re-enter Wi-Fi passwords)."],
  ["wifi connected but no internet | connected no internet access | wifi says no internet", "Your device reached the router, but the router isn't reaching the internet.\n1. Check other devices.\n2. Restart the router and modem (unplug for 30 seconds).\n3. Look at the modem lights for an error.\n4. On a public network, open a browser in case a sign-in page needs accepting.\n5. Contact your provider if it continues."],
  ["bluetooth wont pair | bluetooth device not showing up | bluetooth not connecting", "1. Make sure the device is charged and in **pairing mode**.\n2. Turn Bluetooth off and on.\n3. If it was paired before, **forget** it and pair again.\n4. Disconnect it from other phones or computers nearby.\n5. Restart both devices and keep them close together."],
  ["my phone wont charge | charger not working | phone charging port problems", "1. Try a different cable, charging brick and outlet.\n2. Look into the port with a light; pocket lint often blocks it. Gently clear it with a wooden toothpick, never metal.\n3. Restart the phone.\n4. If it says liquid detected, unplug and let it dry.\n5. Still nothing? The port or battery may need repair."],
  ["an app keeps crashing | app closes by itself | app keeps closing on its own", "1. Force close the app and open it again.\n2. Update the app and your system.\n3. Restart the device.\n4. Clear the app's cache (Android) or offload it (iPhone).\n5. Uninstall and reinstall it. If it's still broken, the developer may need to fix it."],
  ["my touchscreen isnt responding | phone screen frozen | screen not working when i touch it", "1. Wait a moment; it might be busy.\n2. Do a force restart.\n3. Remove the case and screen protector if it's thick or cracked.\n4. Clean the screen and dry your hands.\n5. If parts of the screen never respond, the display may need repair."],
  ["how do i force restart an iphone | force restart android | hard reset phone buttons", "On most iPhones: press and release **volume up**, press and release **volume down**, then hold the **side button** until the logo appears. On most Android phones: hold **power and volume down** for about 10 to 20 seconds. This doesn't delete anything."],
  ["my computer keeps restarting | pc restarts randomly | computer shuts down by itself", "1. Check for overheating; clean dust and vents.\n2. Install Windows and driver updates.\n3. Undo recent changes, like a new driver or program.\n4. Run a malware scan.\n5. If it continues, the power supply, RAM or other hardware may be failing; ask for help."],
  ["monitor says no signal | no display on screen | screen black but computer is on", "1. Check the cable is firmly in at both ends.\n2. Make sure the monitor's **input source** matches the port you used.\n3. Try a different cable or port.\n4. If you have a graphics card, plug the monitor into it.\n5. On Windows, press **Win + Ctrl + Shift + B** to reset the graphics driver."],
  ["my screen is flickering | computer screen flicker | monitor keeps flashing", "1. Reseat or replace the video cable.\n2. Update the graphics driver.\n3. Check the refresh rate in display settings.\n4. If only one app flickers, update or reinstall that app.\n5. If it flickers even in the monitor's own menu, the monitor itself may be faulty."],
  ["print job stuck | printer queue stuck | my printer wont print", "1. Open the print queue and cancel all jobs.\n2. Turn the printer off and on.\n3. Check paper, ink and error lights.\n4. Restart your computer.\n5. Remove and re-add the printer in settings, or reinstall its software from the maker's website."],
  ["my webcam isnt working | camera not working on laptop | camera shows black screen", "1. Check for a privacy shutter or a camera off key.\n2. Close other apps that might be using the camera.\n3. Check privacy settings allow apps to use the camera.\n4. Restart the computer.\n5. Update or reinstall the camera driver."],
  ["my microphone isnt working | people cant hear me | mic not picking up", "1. Check you're not **muted** in the app or by a headset button.\n2. Choose the right microphone in the app's audio settings.\n3. Make sure privacy settings allow microphone access.\n4. Unplug and replug the headset.\n5. Test your mic in system sound settings."],
  ["headphones only work in one ear | one earbud not working | sound only on one side", "1. Check the balance setting in sound or accessibility settings.\n2. Clean the earbud mesh gently.\n3. For wireless earbuds, put both in the case, reset them, and pair again.\n4. For wired ones, try another device; wiggling the cable can reveal a broken wire."],
  ["my controller wont connect | controller not working on pc | gamepad not detected", "1. Charge it or replace the batteries.\n2. Try a different USB cable; some cables only charge.\n3. Put it in pairing mode and pair again.\n4. Update the controller's firmware.\n5. In the game, check that controller input is turned on."],
  ["my game is lagging | game stuttering | how do i get more fps", "1. Close other apps and browser tabs.\n2. Lower graphics settings or resolution.\n3. Update graphics drivers.\n4. Plug laptops into power and use a high-performance mode.\n5. If the problem is **ping** not FPS, use a wired connection and pick a nearer server."],
  ["website wont load on one device | only one site not working | page loads on phone but not computer", "1. Refresh the page or try another browser.\n2. Clear that site's cookies and cache.\n3. Disable extensions, especially ad blockers, for a moment.\n4. Check the date and time on your device are correct.\n5. Restart the device."],
  ["my computer fan is loud | pc fan noise | why is my fan so loud", "Loud fans usually mean the computer is hot or working hard.\n1. Check Task Manager for programs hogging the CPU.\n2. Clean dust from vents and fans with compressed air.\n3. Make sure it has room to breathe.\n4. A clicking or grinding fan may need replacing."],
  ["my keyboard types the wrong symbols | keyboard layout wrong | keys type different characters", "Your keyboard layout probably changed. On Windows, press **Win + Space** to switch layouts, or check Settings → Time & language → Language & region and remove layouts you don't use."],
  ["emails stuck in outbox | my email isnt sending | why wont my email send", "1. Check your internet connection.\n2. Make sure the recipient's address is spelled right.\n3. Remove or shrink large attachments.\n4. Restart the email app.\n5. Sign out and back in to refresh your account."],
  ["i cant hear people on video calls | video call audio problems | no sound in meeting", "1. Check the app's speaker setting and system volume.\n2. Make sure the right output device, like headphones, is selected.\n3. Unmute the app in the volume mixer.\n4. Leave and rejoin the call.\n5. Ask if others can hear them; the problem might be on their end."],
  ["my touchpad isnt working | trackpad stopped working | laptop mouse pad not working", "1. Look for a touchpad on/off key (often an F key) and press it.\n2. Check touchpad settings to make sure it's enabled.\n3. Unplug any external mouse; some laptops disable the touchpad when one is connected.\n4. Restart and update drivers."],
  ["phone stuck on the logo | phone boot loop | phone keeps restarting at logo", "1. Charge it for a while, then force restart.\n2. If it still loops, try the phone's **safe mode** or **recovery mode** (steps differ by phone).\n3. As a last resort, a factory reset may fix it, but it erases data, which is why backups matter. Ask an adult or repair shop for help."],
  ["dropped my phone in water | wet phone what do i do | phone fell in the water", "1. Take it out fast and turn it **off**.\n2. Don't charge it or press lots of buttons.\n3. Wipe it dry and remove the case and SIM tray.\n4. Let it dry in a well-ventilated place for at least a day. Rice doesn't really help, and hairdryers can damage it.\n5. Tell an adult, and get it checked if it acts strangely."],
  ["my phone screen is cracked | cracked screen what do i do | is it safe to use a cracked phone", "Put clear tape or a screen protector over it to stop glass bits from cutting your fingers, and avoid pressing on the cracks. Back up your phone, then get the screen repaired, because cracks can spread and let in dust and water."],
  ["laptop plugged in not charging | laptop battery wont charge | charger connected but not charging", "1. Check the charger is firmly plugged in at both ends and the outlet works.\n2. Look for a battery-saver or charge-limit setting that stops at a certain percent.\n3. Restart the laptop.\n4. Try another compatible charger.\n5. Check battery health; old batteries can stop charging."],
  ["my tv says no signal | tv no signal hdmi | tv shows no input", "1. Make sure the connected device (console, cable box) is turned on.\n2. Press **Input** or **Source** on the remote and pick the right HDMI port.\n3. Reseat the HDMI cable or try another port.\n4. Turn everything off and on again."],
  ["my smart speaker isnt responding | smart speaker offline | voice assistant not working", "1. Check the microphone isn't muted.\n2. Make sure the Wi-Fi is working.\n3. Unplug the speaker for 30 seconds and plug it back in.\n4. Check the companion app for updates or errors.\n5. If the Wi-Fi password changed, set it up again in the app."],
  ["my downloads are slow | download speed is slow | why is my download taking forever", "1. Run a speed test to check your connection.\n2. Pause other downloads, updates and streams.\n3. Move closer to the router or use Ethernet.\n4. The server itself might be busy; try again later.\n5. Restart your router."],
  ["my apps wont update | app store stuck on downloading | app stuck pending", "1. Check your internet connection.\n2. Make sure you have enough free storage.\n3. Restart the device.\n4. Sign out of the app store and back in.\n5. Pause other downloads so one at a time can finish."],
  ["my phone isnt ringing | phone doesnt ring when people call | calls go straight to voicemail", "1. Check that silent mode, do-not-disturb or focus mode is off.\n2. Make sure the ringer volume is up.\n3. Check the number isn't blocked or silenced as unknown callers.\n4. Turn off call forwarding.\n5. Restart the phone."],
  ["auto brightness keeps changing | screen keeps getting darker | what is adaptive brightness", "**Adaptive** or **auto brightness** uses a light sensor to adjust the screen to the room. If it bothers you, turn it off in display settings. Also check battery saver, which can dim the screen."],
  ["my phone keeps showing pop up ads | ads on my phone home screen | adware on android", "1. Note when ads appear, then check recently installed apps and uninstall suspicious ones.\n2. Block notifications from websites in your browser settings.\n3. Run a scan with your phone's built-in security tool.\n4. Only install apps from the official app store."],
  ["my computer clock is wrong | wrong time on my pc | computer time keeps changing", "1. In date and time settings, turn on **Set time automatically** and pick the right time zone.\n2. Click **Sync now**.\n3. If the time resets after unplugging a desktop, the small CMOS battery on the motherboard may need replacing."],
  ["general troubleshooting steps | how do i troubleshoot tech problems | what to try when tech stops working", "A good order to try:\n1. Restart the device.\n2. Check cables, power and connections.\n3. Install updates.\n4. Search the exact error message.\n5. Undo the last thing you changed.\n6. Ask someone for help and describe exactly what happens."],
  ["usb port not working | usb ports stopped working | nothing works in my usb port", "1. Try another device in the same port to see if it's the port or the device.\n2. Restart the computer.\n3. Look inside for dust or bent pins.\n4. On Windows, check Device Manager for warning icons and update USB drivers."],
  ["my phone camera is blurry | camera wont focus | blurry phone photos", "1. Wipe the lens with a soft cloth; fingerprints are the usual cause.\n2. Tap the screen on your subject to focus.\n3. Hold steady or use more light.\n4. Remove a case that may cover the lens.\n5. Restart the camera app."],
  ["my phone says storage full but i deleted stuff | what is system data storage | what is other storage", "Deleted photos often sit in a **Recently deleted** album for up to 30 days, so empty it. **System** or **Other** data includes caches and temporary files; restarting, updating and clearing app caches can shrink it."],
  ["my computer makes a clicking noise | hard drive clicking | strange noises from computer", "A repeated clicking from a hard drive can mean it's failing, so **back up your files right away**. Rattling or buzzing may be a fan hitting a cable. Turn it off and get help if you smell burning."],
  ["laptop wont wake up from sleep | computer stuck in sleep mode | black screen after sleep", "1. Press the power button briefly, or press a key and move the mouse.\n2. Open and close the lid.\n3. If nothing happens, hold the power button for 10 seconds to force it off, then start it again.\n4. Update graphics and chipset drivers to stop it happening again."],
  ["my phone gets no notifications | notifications not showing | not getting app notifications", "1. Check do-not-disturb or focus mode.\n2. In settings, make sure notifications are allowed for that app.\n3. Turn off battery optimization for apps that need instant alerts.\n4. Sign out and back into the app.\n5. Restart the phone."],
  ["my mouse is double clicking by itself | mouse clicks twice | mouse cursor jumping around", "1. Check the battery for wireless mice.\n2. Use a mouse pad on a clean, non-shiny surface.\n3. Clean the sensor underneath.\n4. Try another USB port.\n5. Double-clicking by itself often means the button is wearing out."],
  ["my speakers are crackling | sound is crackling | audio distorted", "1. Turn the volume down; it may be overdriven.\n2. Check and reseat cables.\n3. Update audio drivers.\n4. Try different audio settings or a different output.\n5. If one speaker still buzzes, it might be damaged."],
  ["my phone wont send texts | messages not sending | text failed to send", "1. Check you have signal or Wi-Fi.\n2. Toggle airplane mode on and off.\n3. Make sure the number is correct.\n4. Restart the phone.\n5. Check your plan hasn't run out. If one person never gets them, they may have blocked or changed their number."],
  ["how do i check my privacy settings | privacy settings on apps | how do i make my account private", "Open the app's **Settings → Privacy** (or Account). Good choices: make your account private, limit who can message or comment, turn off location sharing, and review who can find you by phone number or email. Check again after big app updates."],
  ["should my account be private | public vs private account | is it better to have a private account", "For kids and teens, **private** is best. Only people you approve can see your posts, which keeps strangers out. Even then, only accept requests from people you know in real life."],
  ["should i share my location | location sharing apps | how do i turn off location tagging", "Only share your live location with family you trust. Turn off location tags on posts and photos, and don't post pictures that show your school name, house number or street, since they can reveal where you are."],
  ["what are app permissions | why does an app want my camera | should i allow app permissions", "Permissions let apps use things like your camera, microphone, contacts or location. Only allow what makes sense: a camera app needs the camera, but a flashlight app doesn't need your contacts. Choose **while using the app** when possible."],
  ["is a long password better than a complicated one | what is a passphrase | how long should a password be", "Length beats complexity! A **passphrase** of four or more random words, like purple-taco-river-jump, is long, easy to remember and hard to crack. Aim for at least 12 to 16 characters and use a different one for every account."],
  ["is a password manager safe | how do password managers keep passwords safe | can password managers be hacked", "Good password managers **encrypt** your vault so only your main password can unlock it. They're much safer than reusing passwords. Use a strong main password and turn on 2FA for the manager itself."],
  ["what is an authenticator app | authenticator app vs text codes | how do authenticator apps work", "An authenticator app makes a new 6-digit code every 30 seconds for your 2FA logins. It's safer than text-message codes, because scammers can't steal it by hijacking your phone number."],
  ["what is a passkey | passkeys vs passwords | how do passkeys work", "A **passkey** lets you sign in with your fingerprint, face or device PIN instead of a password. It uses a secret key stored on your device that never gets sent to the website, so it can't be phished or leaked."],
  ["what are backup codes | lost my phone with 2fa | i cant get my 2fa code", "When you turn on 2FA, most sites give you **backup codes**. Save them somewhere safe, like a password manager or printed at home. If you lose your phone, a backup code lets you get back in."],
  ["someone asked for my verification code | should i share a 2fa code | someone wants the code sent to my phone", "**Never** share a verification code with anyone, even if they say they're a friend, a game company or support staff. Real companies never ask for it. Someone asking for it is trying to take over your account."],
  ["what is smishing | scam text messages | is this text a scam", "**Smishing** is phishing by text message, like fake delivery notices or prize alerts with a link. Don't tap the link or reply. Check by opening the real app or website yourself, and delete or report the text."],
  ["what is vishing | scam phone calls | someone called saying i owe money", "**Vishing** is phishing by phone call. Scammers pretend to be banks, police or tech support and pressure you to act fast. Hang up, don't give any information, and tell a trusted adult."],
  ["fake giveaway on social media | is this giveaway real | giveaway scam", "Fake giveaways ask you to click links, pay a small fee, or share personal info to claim a prize. Real giveaways never ask you to pay or give passwords. Check that the account is the real verified one before entering."],
  ["how do i recover a hacked account | someone hacked my account | my account got hacked what do i do", "1. Tell a trusted adult.\n2. Use the site's **account recovery** or forgot-password page.\n3. Change the password and log out all other sessions.\n4. Turn on 2FA.\n5. Change the same password anywhere else you used it, and warn friends about any strange messages sent from you."],
  ["what is a data breach | how do i know if my password was leaked | my email was in a breach", "A data breach is when hackers steal information from a company. If a service you use is breached, change that password and anywhere you reused it. Many password managers and breach-check websites can warn you."],
  ["what is identity theft | how do people steal identities", "Identity theft is when someone uses another person's details, like their name, birthday or ID numbers, to pretend to be them. Protect yourself by keeping personal info private and shredding documents with personal details."],
  ["what is catfishing | how do i know if someone online is fake | is this person who they say they are", "**Catfishing** is when someone pretends to be a different person online, often using stolen photos. Warning signs: they won't video chat, their story keeps changing, or they ask for secrets, photos or money. Tell a trusted adult."],
  ["someone online wants to meet me | a stranger online asked me to keep a secret | someone online is asking for photos", "Don't agree, and don't keep it secret. **Tell a trusted adult right away**: a parent, teacher or school counselor. Safe people don't ask kids for secrets, photos or private meetings. You're not in trouble for telling."],
  ["someone is being mean to me online | how do i deal with cyberbullying | what to do if im bullied online", "1. Don't reply or fight back.\n2. Take **screenshots** as evidence.\n3. **Block** and **report** the person.\n4. Tell a trusted adult, like a parent or teacher.\n5. Remember it's not your fault, and you deserve to feel safe."],
  ["how do i block someone online | how do i report someone | how to report a post", "Most apps let you tap the person's profile or the message, then choose **Block** or **Report**. Blocking stops them contacting you, and reporting tells the app's safety team. They usually aren't told who reported them."],
  ["my friend is being cyberbullied | how can i help someone being bullied online | what should i do if i see cyberbullying", "Be an upstander: don't like or share mean posts, send your friend a kind message, help them save evidence, report the posts, and encourage them to tell a trusted adult. If you're worried about their safety, tell an adult yourself."],
  ["someone shared my photo without permission | someone posted a picture of me | how do i get a photo taken down", "Ask the person to remove it, and report the photo to the app; many remove images posted without permission. Tell a trusted adult, especially if the photo is embarrassing or being used to bully you."],
  ["should i ask before posting pictures of friends | is it ok to post photos of other people", "Yes, always ask first! Some people don't want their picture online, and some families have rules about it. Asking before posting shows respect."],
  ["how much screen time is too much | healthy screen time | how much screen time should kids have", "There's no single magic number; what matters is balance. Make sure screens don't push out sleep, homework, outdoor play, exercise and time with family and friends. Many families agree on daily limits and screen-free times like meals and bedtime."],
  ["how do i stop scrolling | i spend too much time on my phone | how to use my phone less", "1. Turn off non-essential notifications.\n2. Set app time limits in your phone's screen time settings.\n3. Move tempting apps off your home screen.\n4. Keep the phone out of your bedroom at night.\n5. Plan offline activities you enjoy."],
  ["should i use my phone before bed | do screens affect sleep | screens and sleep", "Screens before bed can keep your brain alert and delay sleep. Try putting devices away **30 to 60 minutes** before bedtime and charging them outside your bedroom."],
  ["how do parental controls work | what are parental controls | screen time settings for kids", "Parental controls let parents set time limits, filter websites, approve app downloads and block in-app purchases. They're built into most phones, consoles and computers. They work best when families talk about the rules together."],
  ["how do i clean up my digital footprint | can you delete things from the internet | can deleted posts be found", "Delete old posts you regret, tighten privacy settings, close accounts you don't use and search your name to see what shows up. But remember: screenshots and copies can live on, so **think before you post**."],
  ["think before you post | what should i ask myself before posting | is it ok to post this", "Ask yourself: Is it kind? Is it true? Would I be okay with my teacher, grandparent or a future school seeing it? Does it share private info? If you're unsure, don't post it."],
  ["what is misinformation | misinformation vs disinformation | how do i spot misinformation", "**Misinformation** is false info shared by mistake; **disinformation** is false info spread on purpose. Watch for shocking headlines, no named sources, emotional language, and stories that no trusted news outlets are reporting."],
  ["how do i fact check something | how to check if something online is true | what is lateral reading", "1. Pause before sharing.\n2. Check **who** made it and whether they're trustworthy.\n3. Do **lateral reading**: open new tabs and see what other reliable sources say.\n4. Look for the original source and date.\n5. Use fact-checking websites."],
  ["what is clickbait | why do headlines exaggerate", "**Clickbait** is a headline or thumbnail designed to make you curious enough to click, like you won't believe what happened. It often exaggerates or misleads, because more clicks mean more ad money."],
  ["what is a reliable source | how do i know if a source is good", "Reliable sources have named experts, cite evidence, correct their mistakes and aren't just trying to sell something. A **primary source** is original (like a diary or study); a **secondary source** explains or analyzes it."],
  ["how do i tell if an image is ai generated | is this picture real or ai | how to spot an ai image", "Look for odd hands or fingers, warped text, strange backgrounds, too-perfect skin or lighting that doesn't make sense. Check who posted it, look for the original source and use a reverse image search. Better AI makes this harder, so trusted sources matter most."],
  ["what is reverse image search | how do i search with a picture | find where a photo came from", "A reverse image search lets you search using a picture instead of words. It shows where else the image appears online, which helps you find its original source and spot fakes or old photos shared as new."],
  ["what is an echo chamber | what is a filter bubble", "A **filter bubble** happens when apps only show you things similar to what you already like. An **echo chamber** is when you only hear opinions that match yours. Following different trusted sources helps you see the bigger picture."],
  ["what is digital citizenship | how do i be a good digital citizen", "Digital citizenship means using technology safely, kindly and responsibly: protecting your privacy, respecting others, thinking critically about what you read, giving credit for others' work and standing up against bullying."],
  ["what is netiquette | online manners | how to be polite online", "**Netiquette** is good manners online: be kind, don't type in ALL CAPS (it looks like shouting), respect others' privacy, stay on topic and remember there's a real person on the other side of the screen."],
  ["can i use pictures from google | what is copyright online | is it ok to use images i find online", "Most images online are protected by **copyright**, which means the creator owns them. For school projects, use images labeled for reuse, like **Creative Commons** or free stock sites, and give credit."],
  ["what is creative commons | what does cc by mean", "**Creative Commons** licenses let creators share their work with simple rules. For example, **CC BY** means you can use it if you credit the creator. Always check the specific license."],
  ["is copying from the internet cheating | how do i avoid plagiarism", "Plagiarism is using someone else's words or ideas as if they're your own. Avoid it by writing in your own words, using quotation marks for exact quotes and listing your sources."],
  ["fake update pop up | is this update real | website says update your browser", "Real updates come from your device's settings or official app store, not random websites. If a web page says you must download an update, close it and check for updates the normal way."],
  ["should i cover my webcam | can someone spy through my webcam | webcam cover", "Webcam spying is rare, but a cover or sticker is an easy bit of peace of mind. Keeping your device updated, using antivirus and not installing sketchy software are the most important protections."],
  ["is it safe to charge at public usb ports | what is juice jacking | public charging station safe", "Some experts warn that tampered public USB ports could try to access data. To be safe, use your own charger plugged into a power outlet, a power bank, or a charge-only cable."],
  ["does the padlock mean a website is safe | what does the lock icon mean | is https always safe", "The padlock means the connection is **encrypted**, so others can't snoop on it. It does **not** prove the site is honest; scam sites can have padlocks too. Check the web address carefully."],
  ["how do i avoid accidental in app purchases | what are in app purchases | what are loot boxes", "In-app purchases let you buy things inside apps and games. **Loot boxes** are paid mystery rewards. Ask a parent before buying anything, and families can require a password or approval for every purchase in settings."],
  ["online gaming safety tips | is voice chat with strangers safe | gaming chat safety", "1. Use a nickname that doesn't reveal your real name or age.\n2. Never share personal info, passwords or account details.\n3. Use privacy settings to limit chat to friends.\n4. Mute, block and report anyone mean or creepy.\n5. Tell a trusted adult if something feels wrong."],
  ["what is doxxing | what does dox mean", "**Doxxing** is when someone finds and posts another person's private information, like their address or school, without permission. It's harmful and against the rules on most platforms. Protect yourself by keeping personal details private and report it to a trusted adult if it happens."],
  ["what is a strong pin | is 1234 a good pin | how do i choose a phone passcode", "Avoid simple PINs like 1234, 0000 or your birthday. A 6-digit or longer PIN with no obvious pattern is much stronger, and don't let others watch you type it."],
  ["what should i do before getting rid of an old phone | how to wipe a phone | selling an old computer", "1. Back up photos and files.\n2. Sign out of all accounts and turn off find-my-device features.\n3. Remove the SIM and memory card.\n4. Do a **factory reset** to erase everything.\n5. Recycle electronics properly instead of throwing them in the trash."],
  ["how do i recycle old electronics | can you throw batteries in the trash", "Old gadgets and batteries are **e-waste** and contain materials that can harm the environment or start fires in trash trucks. Take them to an electronics recycling drop-off or a store take-back program."],
  ["what is find my device | how do i find my lost phone | locate my phone", "Find-my-device features let you see your phone's location on a map, make it ring, lock it or erase it remotely. Turn it on in settings before you lose it, and sign in on another device or website to use it."],
  ["how does a chatbot understand me | how do ai chatbots answer questions | how does an ai chat know what to say", "An AI chatbot has learned patterns from huge amounts of text. When you type, it predicts, word by word, what a helpful reply would look like. It doesn't understand like a person does, which is why it can sound confident and still be wrong. (I'm a simpler offline helper that matches your question to answers I already have.)"],
  ["how does ai learn | how is ai trained | how do you teach an ai", "AI learns from **examples**. It makes a guess, checks how wrong it was, and adjusts millions of tiny internal numbers to do a little better next time. After repeating this a huge number of times, it gets good at spotting patterns."],
  ["neural network explained simply | how does a neural network learn | how do neural networks work for kids", "Imagine lots of tiny decision-makers connected in layers. Each one passes a signal forward, and the connections have strengths. During training, those strengths get nudged until the network's answers match the right answers more often, a bit like practicing until you get it."],
  ["how do ai image generators work | how does ai make pictures | what is a diffusion model", "Many AI image generators start with pure random noise and remove it step by step, guided by your text description, until a picture appears. They learned how by studying huge numbers of images paired with captions."],
  ["why does ai make mistakes | can ai be wrong | what is an ai hallucination", "Yes! AI predicts likely answers rather than looking up facts, so it can make things up and sound sure about it; that's called a **hallucination**. It can also be out of date or misunderstand you. Always double-check important info."],
  ["what is a deepfake | how can i spot a deepfake | are deepfakes illegal", "A **deepfake** is a video, image or voice faked with AI to make it look like someone said or did something they didn't. Watch for odd blinking, mouth movements that don't match the words, blurry edges and strange lighting, and check if trusted sources report it. Making harmful deepfakes of real people can be illegal."],
  ["can ai be biased | what is ai bias | why is ai unfair sometimes", "AI learns from data made by people, so if the data is unbalanced or contains unfair ideas, the AI can repeat them. That's **bias**. Developers try to reduce it by testing carefully and using more varied data."],
  ["what is generative ai | what does generative ai mean", "**Generative AI** creates new content, like text, images, music, code or video, based on patterns it learned from examples. Chatbots and AI image makers are both generative AI."],
  ["what is a prompt | how do i write a good prompt | prompt tips", "A **prompt** is what you type to tell an AI what you want. Good prompts are specific: say the topic, who it's for, the length and style. For example: Write a 3-sentence fun fact about owls for a 4th grader."],
  ["is it okay to use ai for homework | is using ai cheating | can i use chatbots for school", "It depends on your teacher's rules, so ask! AI can be a great helper for brainstorming, explaining ideas or quizzing yourself. Turning in AI writing as your own work usually counts as cheating, and you'd miss out on learning."],
  ["is ai art stealing | can ai copy art | is ai art real art", "People disagree about this. AI image tools learned from lots of existing art, and some artists feel that's unfair without permission, while others see AI as a new creative tool. Laws about it are still developing. Being honest about when you used AI is always a good idea."],
  ["how does speech recognition work | how does my phone understand my voice | voice to text", "Your voice is recorded as a sound wave, split into tiny pieces, and an AI model trained on lots of speech figures out which sounds and words they most likely are. It then uses context to pick the best sentence."],
  ["how does the algorithm know what i like | what is a recommendation algorithm | how does youtube know what to show me", "Recommendation algorithms track what you watch, like, skip and search, then suggest things that similar viewers enjoyed. They're designed to keep you watching, so it's good to notice when you've been scrolling a long time."],
  ["what is facial recognition | how does facial recognition work", "Facial recognition measures features like the distance between your eyes and the shape of your jaw, turning your face into a kind of number code. It compares that code to saved ones to find a match. It's used for unlocking phones and tagging photos."],
  ["how do self driving cars work | what is a self driving car | are self driving cars safe", "Self-driving cars use cameras, radar and sometimes lidar (laser sensors) to see the road. AI software identifies cars, people and signs, and decides how to steer, brake and accelerate. Many still need a human ready to take over."],
  ["what is an ai model | what are parameters in ai | how big are ai models", "An **AI model** is the trained system that makes predictions. Its **parameters** are the internal numbers adjusted during training. Large models can have billions of them, which helps them pick up more complex patterns."],
  ["what is a token in ai | what are tokens in chatbots", "AI language models read text in chunks called **tokens**: whole words, parts of words or punctuation. A long word might be split into several tokens. Models have limits on how many tokens they can handle at once."],
  ["does ai remember what i say | does ai save my chats | is it safe to tell a chatbot personal info", "It depends on the service: some save conversations and may use them to improve their AI. So treat chatbots like a public place and **don't share** personal details like your full name, address, school or passwords."],
  ["what is ai voice cloning | ai voice scam | can ai copy someone's voice", "AI can copy a voice from short recordings. Scammers sometimes use fake voices pretending to be a family member in trouble. Families can agree on a **secret code word** to check if a call is really them."],
  ["how do i use ai safely | ai safety tips for kids", "1. Don't share personal info.\n2. Double-check facts with trusted sources.\n3. Follow your school's rules about AI.\n4. Don't use AI to make fake images of real people.\n5. Tell a trusted adult if an AI says something upsetting."],
  ["what is ocr | how does my phone read text in pictures | copy text from an image", "**OCR** (optical character recognition) lets a computer find and read letters in an image. Phones use it so you can copy text from a photo, translate signs or scan documents."],
  ["how does a translation app work | how does machine translation work | is ai translation accurate", "Translation apps use AI trained on huge collections of texts in different languages. They translate whole sentences using context, not word by word. They're very useful but can miss jokes, slang and cultural meaning."],
  ["can ai feel emotions | does ai have feelings | is ai my friend", "No. AI can produce words that sound friendly or emotional, but it doesn't actually feel anything. It's fine to enjoy chatting with AI, but real friends and trusted adults are the ones to turn to for support."],
  ["what jobs use ai | how is ai used in real life | examples of ai in everyday life", "AI helps with spam filters, map directions, photo editing, video recommendations, voice assistants, weather forecasts, spotting diseases in medical scans and much more, usually quietly in the background."],
  ["how old do you have to be for social media | social media age limit | why do you have to be 13 for social media", "Most big social media apps require users to be at least **13**, partly because of child privacy laws, and some places set higher ages. Some apps have special versions for younger kids. Always check with a parent first."],
  ["how does social media work | what is social media | why do people use social media", "Social media apps let people share posts, photos and videos and follow others. Your feed is chosen by an algorithm based on what you interact with. It's fun for staying connected and creating, but it's built to keep you scrolling."],
  ["what are followers | what are likes | why do likes matter so much", "**Followers** are people who see your posts, and **likes** show someone enjoyed one. Likes can feel exciting, but they don't measure your worth; many apps let you hide like counts."],
  ["what does going viral mean | how do videos go viral", "Going **viral** means a post spreads super fast as many people share it. It's often unpredictable, and once something goes viral, you can't take it back, which is another reason to think before posting."],
  ["what is an influencer | are influencers paid | what is a sponsored post", "An **influencer** is someone with a big following who shares content, and companies often pay them to promote products. Look for labels like **Ad**, **Sponsored** or **Paid partnership**, and remember they're being paid to say good things."],
  ["what is a hashtag | how do hashtags work", "A hashtag is a word or phrase after a # sign, like #science. Clicking it shows other posts using the same tag, which helps people find content on a topic."],
  ["do disappearing messages really disappear | are snaps really gone | what are stories on social media", "**Stories** and **disappearing messages** vanish after a while, but anyone can screenshot or record them with another phone first. Treat everything you send as possibly permanent."],
  ["what is a dm | what are direct messages", "A **DM** (direct message) is a private message between users on social media. Only accept DMs from people you know, and tell a trusted adult if a stranger messages you."],
  ["social media makes me feel bad | comparing myself to others online | how do i deal with fomo", "Remember that people usually post their **highlights**, often edited or filtered, not their everyday life. Unfollow accounts that make you feel bad, take breaks and spend time with people offline. Talk to someone you trust if it's getting you down."],
  ["what is a meme | what does meme mean", "A **meme** is a funny image, video or phrase that spreads online and gets remixed by lots of people. Good memes are fun, but avoid ones that make fun of real people in mean ways."],
  ["how do i pick a safe username | good username ideas | what should my username be", "Pick a fun nickname that doesn't include your full name, age, birthday, school or location. Something like a favorite animal plus a random word works well."],
  ["what is a verified account | what does a blue check mean | is a verified account real", "A checkmark badge usually means the account was confirmed as genuine or, on some apps, that it pays for a subscription. It helps spot impersonators, but always check the username carefully too."],
  ["fps in games | what is a good frame rate for gaming | 30 vs 60 vs 120 fps", "FPS (frames per second) is how many images a game draws each second. **30 fps** is playable, **60 fps** feels smooth, and **120+ fps** feels super responsive on a high refresh rate screen. Higher FPS needs a more powerful device."],
  ["lag vs low fps | why do games lag | what causes lag in online games", "Two different problems: **low FPS** means your device can't draw frames fast enough, so the game looks choppy. **Network lag** means data is slow getting to the server, so characters teleport or actions are delayed. Fix FPS with settings; fix lag with a better connection."],
  ["what is input lag | why does my game feel delayed | input delay", "Input lag is the delay between pressing a button and seeing it happen on screen. TV processing, low frame rates and wireless connections add to it. Turning on the TV's **game mode** helps a lot."],
  ["how do game controllers work | what is an analog stick | how does a joystick work", "Buttons complete a tiny circuit when pressed. Analog sticks measure how far and in what direction you push them using sensors, so you can walk slowly or run. The controller sends all this to the console many times per second."],
  ["what is stick drift | controller drifting | why does my character move by itself", "**Stick drift** is when an analog stick reports movement even when you aren't touching it, usually due to wear or dirt. Try calibrating the controller, cleaning around the stick, or adjusting the **deadzone** in game settings. Repair or replacement may be needed."],
  ["what is haptic feedback in controllers | what is rumble | what are adaptive triggers", "**Rumble** shakes the controller for things like explosions. Advanced **haptics** create detailed vibrations, like feeling raindrops, and **adaptive triggers** push back to feel like pulling a bowstring."],
  ["what is cloud gaming | how does cloud gaming work | what is game streaming", "In cloud gaming, the game runs on a powerful computer in a data center. It streams the video to your screen, and your button presses are sent back. You can play big games on a phone or cheap laptop, but you need fast, steady internet."],
  ["what internet do i need for cloud gaming | why does cloud gaming lag", "Cloud gaming typically wants at least 15 to 25 Mbps for good quality, more for 4K, and low, stable ping. Use 5 GHz Wi-Fi close to the router or a wired connection for the best experience."],
  ["what is a game server | what is matchmaking | what is a dedicated server", "A game server runs the shared online world and keeps all players in sync. **Matchmaking** puts players of similar skill or region into the same game. **Dedicated servers** are run by the game company rather than a player's console."],
  ["why are game updates so big | what is a game patch | why do games need updates", "A **patch** fixes bugs, balances gameplay or adds content. Updates can be huge because they often replace big files like textures, maps and sounds, especially for games with high-quality graphics."],
  ["what is crossplay | what does cross platform mean | can pc and console play together", "**Crossplay** means players on different systems, like PC, consoles and phones, can play together online. **Cross-progression** lets your progress follow you between devices. Not every game supports them."],
  ["gaming laptop vs desktop | what is a gaming pc | do i need a gaming pc", "A gaming PC has a strong graphics card, good cooling and a high refresh rate screen. **Desktops** are more powerful for the money and upgradable; **gaming laptops** are portable but louder, hotter and harder to upgrade."],
  ["what do graphics settings do | what is anti aliasing | what is texture quality", "Graphics settings trade looks for speed. **Texture quality** sets surface detail, **shadows** and **lighting** add realism, and **anti-aliasing** smooths jagged edges. Lowering shadows and effects usually boosts FPS most."],
  ["what is vsync | should i turn on vsync | vsync on or off", "**V-Sync** makes the game wait for the screen's refresh to stop tearing, but it can add input lag. If your screen supports adaptive sync (G-Sync or FreeSync), that's usually the better choice."],
  ["what is upscaling in games | what is dlss | what is fsr", "Upscaling renders the game at a lower resolution and uses smart algorithms or AI to sharpen it to a higher one. It boosts frame rates while still looking good. DLSS, FSR and XeSS are different versions of this idea."],
  ["what is a handheld gaming pc | what is a portable console", "A handheld gaming PC is a full computer squeezed into a device with built-in controls and screen, so you can play PC games on the go. Battery life is shorter when running demanding games."],
  ["what are game mods | what is modding | are game mods safe", "**Mods** are fan-made changes to a game, like new items, maps or characters. Download them only from trusted, well-known mod sites, read reviews, and ask an adult before installing, since bad files can contain malware."],
  ["do i need a gaming headset | what is a gaming headset", "A gaming headset combines headphones and a microphone, helpful for team chat and hearing directions of footsteps. Any comfortable headphones plus a decent mic can work just as well."],
  ["what is backward compatibility | can new consoles play old games", "**Backward compatibility** means a new console can play games from older ones. Many consoles support some or all games from the previous generation, but check the maker's list for each game."],
  ["digital vs physical games | should i buy digital or physical games", "**Physical** discs or cartridges can be lent, traded or resold. **Digital** games are convenient, never get scratched and download straight to your console, but use storage space and can't usually be resold."],
  ["why are games so big | how much storage do games take | why do games take up so much space", "Modern games include high-resolution textures, detailed 3D models, voices in many languages and hours of music and video, so big ones can take tens of gigabytes or more."],
  ["what is a game engine used for | what is unity | what is unreal engine", "Game engines like Unity, Unreal and Godot provide ready-made tools for graphics, physics, sound and controls, so creators can focus on designing the game. Many are free to start learning."],
  ["what is aim assist | what is sensitivity in games | how do i aim better", "**Aim assist** gently helps controller players keep aim on targets. **Sensitivity** controls how fast the camera turns. Finding a comfortable sensitivity and practicing regularly helps aim more than buying new gear."],
  ["what is rubber banding in games | why does my character teleport back", "**Rubber banding** happens when your game and the server disagree about where you are, often due to lag or packet loss, so the server snaps you back. A wired connection and closing downloads help."],
  ["how do i take breaks while gaming | gaming too long | is gaming bad for you", "Gaming can be fun, social and even good for problem-solving. Just keep a balance: take a stretch break every hour, drink water, keep good posture, and make sure it doesn't cut into sleep, homework or time outside."]
);
